# Changelog

All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).

## [Unreleased]

### Added
- added per-module install/uninstall targets throughout the tree
- added sqlrsh json/jsonl output, full command/option coverage of the
  client apis, -continueonerror, -fieldsas, and distinct exit
  codes
- added the tds_connectstrings and firebird_connectstrings auth modules
- added sqlrauth::challenge() for protocol modules to call
- odbc connection module: added binarylobbind, implemented
  nextResultSet(), fixed blob binding and heap corruption,
  improved type reporting, exposed output binds/params
- added translate-bind-variable and read-only/isolation-level controls to
  sqlrservercursor/sqlrservercontroller
- added SQLR_ERROR_MAXCOLUMNCOUNTTOOSMALL for an undersized maxcolumncount
- added columnInfoIsValidAfterPrepare() to db2cursor/informixcursor
- added sqlrservercontroller::isResumedSession()
- added backrefs to the pattern query/error translations, and fixed a
  zero-length-match hang and a capture-group bug in them
- added a sid listener attribute and maxloginattempts retry limit to the
  oracle protocol module
- added debug tracing to the oracle and tds protocol modules, and brought
  postgresql's debug output to parity with mysql's
- added -query to sqlrsh, for running a single sql statement, with no
  delimiter scan
- added -agent to sqlrsh, a preset for -quiet -format jsonl
  -nullsasnulls on -continueonerror on
- added -batch to sqlrsh, for reading piped-in commands with no banner
  or prompts, counting failures like -script and -command do
- added "help brief" to sqlrsh, a shorter, curated command reference
  alongside the existing full "help"
- added "help json" to sqlrsh, a machine-readable command catalog
  (name, args, description) for programmatic discovery
- added "vertical" to sqlrsh, a plain-format display mode that prints
  one column per line, labeled with the column name, for
  result sets too wide for the table layout
- added "pager" to sqlrsh, which pipes plain-format result sets through
  $PAGER, less or more during an interactive session
- added "show schema [like 'pattern']" to sqlrsh, which dumps every
  matching table and its columns in one call, nested per table
  in json/jsonl and one result set per table in plain/csv
- added tab completion to sqlrsh, which completes sqlrsh commands, sql
  keywords, table names and column names during an interactive
  session
- added pre-tds7 (tds 5.0) login support to the tds protocol module, so
  sap's native open client ct-lib - which always negotiates tds
  5.0 - can log in, rather than being turned away by an
  unimplemented-feature error; error, info, envchange and login
  ack tokens are encoded with single-byte strings instead of
  ucs-2 on that path now, and tds 5.0 data requests are refused
  with a readable message instead of a dropped connection
- added tds 5.0 language-command decoding to the tds protocol module, so
  a query from sap's native open client ct-lib is actually run
  rather than being refused - the tds 5.0 "normal" buffer is
  token-framed rather than carrying bare ucs-2, and the sql
  inside its language token is single-byte and not nul
  terminated; a command that returns no result set is answered
  properly now
- added tds 5.0 result-set encoding to the tds protocol module, so a
  query from sap's native open client ct-lib now gets a real
  result set back instead of an error - the ms-tds colmetadata
  encoding could not be reused, since its 0x81 token byte means
  cursor-delete rather than column metadata in tds 5.0; tds 5.0
  gets its own rowfmt and row tokens, varint length classes,
  null encoding, and type map, since the dialects disagree on
  which types exist and what several byte values mean; column
  names are single-byte instead of ucs-2, decimal and numeric
  values use the opposite sign convention and a big-endian
  magnitude, and a command that returns more than one result set
  now advances through all of them instead of stopping after the
  first
- added tds 5.0 parameter decoding to the tds protocol module, so a
  parameterized language command is bound and run now rather
  than being refused - the values ride in a paramfmt/params pair
  behind the language token, and a params token carries no
  lengths of its own, so the paramfmt in front of it has to be
  replayed to size every value; the parameters are named in the
  sql text, the way an ms-tds sp_executesql call names them, so
  bind variable translation pairs them up by position
- added tds 5.0 remote procedure calls to the tds protocol module, so a
  procedure called from sap's native open client runs now - the
  reply is a result set, then a return status, then the output
  parameters as a paramfmt/params pair, since tds 5.0 has no
  returnvalue token at all; the procedure dispatch is shared
  with the ms-tds path rather than copied, but the five cursor
  procedures are refused, because they write a colmetadata
  token, and that byte means cursor-delete in tds 5.0
- added tds 5.0 dynamic sql to the tds protocol module, so prepare,
  execute, deallocate and execute-immediate from sap's native
  open client work now - statements are named by a string id
  rather than the number the ms-tds path uses, and a prepare is
  answered with the input parameter formats and the output
  column formats together, which is why a client never asks to
  describe either one; the client wraps a prepare in a "create
  proc" that would leave a real procedure behind on the server,
  so that wrapper is stripped
- added tds 5.0 remote procedure calls to the tds protocol module - a
  dbrpc token names its procedure by string rather than by the
  numeric id an ms-tds rpc uses, so the name is mapped back to
  an id and both dialects run the same procedure dispatch; the
  reply is a rowfmt/rows result set, a return status, the output
  parameters as a paramfmt/params pair, and a done, which is
  what a real ase sends
- added tds 5.0 dynamic sql to the tds protocol module - prepare,
  execute, deallocate and execute-immediate, all four answered
  with the ack a real ase sends; a dynamic statement is named by
  a string id the client picks rather than by the numeric handle
  ms-tds mints, so a map of one onto the other sits beside the
  handle maps, and prepare, execute and unprepare now share a
  wire-neutral core with the ms-tds sp_prepare, sp_execute and
  sp_unprepare procedures
- the tds protocol module supports tds 5.0 encrypted login now - a
  ct-lib client with CS_SEC_ENCRYPTION sends no password at all
  and waits for the server to drive a key exchange, which is
  answered with a login ack status of negotiate and the
  SEC_ENCRYPT/SEC_LOGPWD/SEC_REMPWD messages a real ase uses;
  the password cipher isn't documented anywhere and freetds
  only implements the newer rsa scheme, so it's taken from the
  open client library the connection already links, and an
  instance without one still refuses such logins with a real
  error rather than letting an empty password through
- added the keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg query translation module, to
  rewrite oracle's max/min(x) keep (dense_rank first/last order
  by y) into a plain postgresql aggregate
- added sqlrservercontroller::getNativeDbType(), and fixed the
  globaltemptables, normalize, and singlestep modules to use it
  instead of getDbType() where they were deciding how to talk to
  the real backend - getDbType() follows a dbtype= override, a
  normal thing to set during a migration to make one database
  impersonate another for the client's benefit, and following
  that override for a backend-behavior decision silently picks
  the wrong behavior for the real database being talked to
- added tds 5.0 cursors to the tds protocol module, so declare,
  set-rows, open, fetch, close, deallocate and positioned update
  and delete from sap's native open client work now - each
  cursor token is acked separately, the way a real ase does it,
  so a batched declare/rows/open comes back as two acks and then
  the row format, which is what makes ct-lib report a cursor
  result rather than a third command-succeeded; a cursor is
  named by a string the client picks and by an id the server
  mints, so a map of one onto the other sits beside the dynamic
  statement map; a cursor open carries its parameters as a
  paramfmt/params pair; and cursor update and delete drive the
  same positioned dml the ms-tds sp_cursor procedure does,
  through a wire-neutral core split out of it
- added -libexecdir and -bindir runtime overrides to sqlr-listener,
  sqlr-connection, sqlr-scaler, sqlr-start and sqlrsh, so the
  module search path and server binary directory no longer have
  to be the compiled-in ones; added a "make stagemodules
  STAGEDIR=..." target that collects a build's modules into one
  directory for use with -libexecdir; the protocol test suites
  can now run against a build tree with no install (test.sh
  -buildtree) and pick their own free ports to avoid colliding
  with a concurrent run (test.sh -randomports)
- added setCursorName()/getCursorName() for "where current of"
  positioned update/delete support: the server api
  (sqlrservercursor) and a new supports_set_cursor_name db
  feature; backend support in firebird (closing and
  re-executing an already-open cursor when a name arrives
  after the fact), freetds, sap, db2, informix and the odbc
  connection module; wire support in the sqlrclient, firebird
  and tds protocol modules; not supported on oracle, mysql,
  sqlite or postgresql, whose PQprepare/PQexecPrepared
  execution strategy and faked autocommit handling make a
  real DECLARE CURSOR path too costly for this pass
- added the matching setCursorName()/getCursorName() calls to the c and
  c++ client apis, and passthroughs in the perl, python, php,
  ruby, tcl, erlang and nodejs wrappers, the odbc driver, and
  the jdbc driver (Statement.setCursorName()/
  ResultSet.getCursorName() were previously stubbed to throw)
- the oracle protocol module now implements commit, rollback,
  autocommit on/off and commit-on-success (OCI_COMMIT_ON_SUCCESS)
  instead of answering them as unimplemented
- added SQLSTATE support - the connection module interface gained
  getSqlState() alongside getError(), and the server captures
  it with the error and carries it through the session; the
  postgresql, mysql, odbc, db2, informix, firebird, sap and
  router connection modules supply a real value, other
  backends report an empty string until their modules
  implement it; the sqlrclient error response carries it as
  a new trailing field, on protocol version 4, which the
  null blob/clob bind change above already bumped to
- the client apis expose the sqlstate on both the connection and the
  cursor as errorSqlState() - c, c++, cs, java, nodejs, perl,
  php, python, ruby, tcl and erlang - and report an empty
  string when the backend supplied none
- the globaltemptables trigger's alreadyexists tag now also supports a
  sqlstate attribute, checked in addition to the existing error
  attribute, with a built-in default per database (postgresql
  42P07, mysql/mariadb 42S01), so already-exists recognition no
  longer depends on the database's error language
- the upsert and replay triggers can now also match on sqlstate: an
  <error> tag in the upsert trigger accepts a sqlstate attribute
  independent of its existing string and number attributes, and a
  <condition> tag in the replay trigger accepts a sqlstate attribute
  as an alternative to its existing error attribute; the db2
  connection module's liveconnection detection also now accepts a
  sqlstate as an alternative to its error-size/text checks for its
  SQL1224N and CLI0106E cases (SQL30081N has no documented sqlstate,
  so it's unchanged)
- added a bigchunkclr attribute to the oracle protocol module's listener,
  which overrides the big chunk clr framing negotiation.  the
  default, auto, negotiates as before.  off never advertises
  CCAP_TTC3 bit 0x20 and always frames each long clr chunk's
  length as a raw byte, for a client like go-ora v1 that
  advertises the bit but never implemented big chunks, and which
  nothing on the wire tells apart from go-ora v2, which did.  on
  forces big chunks for any tti 6 session, whatever the client
  advertises
- added a date_to_text_format connect string parameter to the oracle
  connection module.  when it's set, date columns are fetched
  as oracle dates and converted to text with the given format,
  client-side, so a date keeps its time of day.  when it's
  unset, the default, date columns are fetched as text using
  the session's nls_date_format, exactly as before.  the
  parameter neither reads nor sets nls_date_format, so nothing
  about the session moves and untyped date literals keep
  parsing as they did
- added a configure check for oracle's oci7 legacy call interface:
  looks for the ocidfn.h/ociapr.h/ocikpr.h headers under
  $ORACLE_HOME/rdbms/demo or rdbms/public, which only ship
  with a full oracle client install and never with instant
  client, then confirms the olog/oopen/oparse/odefin/oexec/
  ofen/ofetch/oexfet/oclose/ologof/odescr/obndrv symbols link;
  sets HAVE_OCI7 in config.h and a HAVE_OCI7 build flag in
  config.mk when present, as an optional capability separate
  from the main oracle probe
- added an O3LOGON authentication verifier to the oracle protocol and
  oracle userlist/connectstrings auth modules, selected with
  verifiertype="9i", so a pre-10g OCI7 client can log in at all.
  O5LOGON postdates OCI7 entirely, and such a client answers an
  O5LOGON challenge by dropping the connection. the crypto - the
  oracle DES password hash, the SHA-1 3DES key derivation, and
  the obfuscated password buffer - is verified byte for byte
  against real captures of an Oracle9i client logging into a real
  10.2 server, both a successful login and a rejected one
- the oracle protocol module implements tti osql7 (0x4a) now - the
  pre-8.0 parse call an OCI7 client's oparse puts on the wire,
  where an OCI8 client sends query, query2 or query3. it used
  to answer ORA-03001, unimplemented feature, so an OCI7 client
  could log in and open a cursor but never run anything
- added .gitignore coverage for build output under src/, which had none -
  a built tree showed around 1175 untracked files that a careless
  "git add src/" could sweep into a commit
- added .gitignore coverage for the configure and build output at the repo
  root and in bin/ and init/, which had none - config.mk,
  config.h, libtool, the .pc files, autom4te.cache/ and the
  generated sqlr*-config, rc.*, *.plist and *.service files
- added support for the oracle protocol module's classic,
  pre-session-key login (TTI_LOGON_PRESENT_USER/
  TTI_LOGON_PRESENT_PWD) that an ancient pre-8.0 OCI client's
  olog() call sends, the only shape that interface ever uses -
  a fixed sequence of positional pointer/length fields
  mirroring olog()'s own argument list, followed by one
  contiguous, undelimited run of the strings those fields
  describe; also fixed the session key challenge response's
  bare-buffer shape (used only for this login format) to stop
  writing a redundant length byte on top of the count that
  already precedes it, which broke every such login right
  after the challenge
- the oracle protocol module now implements the oci7 describe call
  (TTI_DESCRIBE) instead of refusing it with ORA-03001, so an
  oci7 client's odescr() works: it answers with every column in
  the select list at once - type, size, precision, scale, null
  flag, character set and name - in either encoding, and answers
  a position past the end of the select list with ORA-01007;
  decoded byte for byte from real oci7 sessions against a live
  10.2 backend
- added a configure check (FW_CHECK_OCIROWIDTOCHAR) for OCIRowidToChar,
  which some early OCI8-era oracle clients (Oracle 9.0.1 on
  solaris/sparc and 9.2.0.4 on redhat/x86 both surfaced this)
  export under a short platform-specific alias with no
  prototype for either name in any header, even though the
  rest of the OCI8 interface is present and the symbol itself
  is genuinely linkable; compiles and links the exact call the
  real code makes, through oci.h alone, and sets
  HAVE_OCIROWIDTOCHAR in config.h only where that call
  actually works
- oracle protocol module: oci7 clients can use bind variables now - a
  query2 request's binds ride inline in the request, behind the
  define descriptors, rather than arriving as the separate
  follow-up packets a pre-8i wiki page described; the old
  two-phase handling answered with bytes no real client could
  parse, so every bind-carrying oci7 session died with ORA-03113
  on the execute, and a bind request's defines were discarded as
  leftover bytes on top of that; in binds of number and character
  types, including nulls, are decoded from real captures now,
  while pl/sql out and in-out binds, and bind values wider than
  252 bytes, are still refused; a dml executed this way reports
  the rows it affected instead of always reporting 0, and a
  client that binds once and executes many times now has its
  later executes' values read too, though the answer such an
  execute gets is still a hardcoded one it may not accept

### Changed
- requires rudiments 2.3.0 or newer
- make install now writes each file under a temp name and renames it, so a
  running listener or connection isn't disrupted
- documented sqlrsh fully on the website; added a README.md for the github
  mirror
- the website now builds from expanded server-side includes instead of
  relying on apache; doc html files install 644 now
- rewrote the oracle userlist auth module for real o5logon verification
  and fixed its one-way password encryption handling; moved
  o5logon's sha-512/aes onto rudiments
- tds protocol module generally works now - freetds, ct-lib, odbc and jdbc
  clients can log in, run queries, use rpc, cursors, bulk load and
  output parameters, and cancel; fixed an authentication bypass
  and a pile of pre-auth buffer overruns and unbounded allocations
- bumped the sqlrclient protocol to version 3 (extra result sets carry
  their column metadata and rows now); fixed
  sqlrcursor::nextResultSet() not refreshing cached
  metadata/buffers, which broke odbc, php pdo and sqlrsh
- date/time reformatting now leaves an unformatted field alone
- sap connection module: accurate column type/width reporting
- oracle protocol module generally works against modern clients now -
  ojdbc, python-oracledb, node-oracledb and oci can connect, log
  in and run queries; fixed an authentication bypass, user
  enumeration, and a pile of pre-auth unbounded allocations and
  oob reads
- firebird protocol module generally works now - connect, attach, prepare,
  execute, fetch, commit and detach, plus blobs, arrays, slices,
  batch ops, and protocol 13+ with SRP auth and Arc4 wire
  encryption; fixed an authentication bypass and a pile of
  pre-auth bugs
- postgresql protocol module: real column type reporting, lob/bytea
  support, fixed pre/post-auth oob reads and bind leaks
- firebird connection module: describes result sets at prepare time; fixed
  DATE/DECIMAL/NUMERIC rendering, error message and leak/crash
  bugs
- session start queries now run for mysql, postgresql, tds, oracle,
  teradata and firebird protocol clients
- documented the php pdo driver's dsn options
- tds protocol test suite split into tds-freetds/tds-sap binaries, with
  matching configure and windows build support; fixed tds/sap
  login negotiation, date/time and decimal bulk load bugs, and
  windows never building tds-sap.exe
- java api: getColumnNames() avoids an unneeded allocation
- firebird protocol module: proper errors for service manager ops and
  unparseable blr, instead of dropping the connection
- documented that oracle_clear_password has no reachable caller
- rewrote the oracle_connectstrings auth module to speak O5LOGON
- cleaned up source code comments in the tds, firebird and oracle protocol
  modules and their auth modules
- moved the oracle protocol module's length-prefixed integer read/write
  into sqlrprotocol as readLenPreInt()/writeLenPreInt(), next to
  readBerEncInt()/writeBerEncInt()
- documented the mysql protocol module's handshake listener attribute in
  the config guide - the legacy protocol version 9 handshake it
  switches to, and that any value other than 10 selects it
- reworked the tds and oracle protocol sections of the config guide to
  follow the same structure as the mysql and postgresql
  sections - basic configuration, listener options,
  authentication/encryption options, foreign backend - and added
  matching example config files under doc/admin/parts/
- the sqlrclient protocol now rejects a client-declared protocol version
  it doesn't support (SQLR_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED_PROTOCOL), including
  a client that never declares a version at all, instead of
  silently accepting any version
- the odbc connection module now runs bind-free statements against ms
  sql server with SQLExecDirect rather than prepare+execute, so a
  local #temp table and session-scoped SET options such as SET
  LOCK_TIMEOUT survive to the next statement instead of dying with
  the sp_prepexec batch that created them; statements with bind
  variables still use prepare+execute, and executedirect=no in the
  connect string restores the old behavior everywhere
- the odbc connection module now recognizes ms sql server local temp
  tables and drops them at session end, so they no longer outlive
  the client session on a pooled connection
- the tds protocol module reads partially length prefixed parameter
  values now, so a varchar(max), varbinary(max), nvarchar(max) or
  xml parameter no longer desyncs the parameter stream and fails
  with "Data type 0x00 is unknown" - which is how text and image
  parameters arrive under tds 7.2 and up
- the tds protocol module reads partially length prefixed bulk copy
  values now, so a bulk copy into a varchar(max),
  varbinary(max), nvarchar(max) or xml column no longer
  misreads the column's 0xFFFF max length as a real length and
  dies with "Malformed bulk load row" - the same fix the rpc
  parameter path already got, applied to the bulk load path
- a null blob or clob bind now reaches the connection module's blob/clob
  bind rather than arriving as a plain null character bind, so
  modules can bind it as the right type; bumped the sqlrclient
  protocol to version 4 for it - an older client still works
  against a newer server, but a version 4 client is rejected at
  connect time by a server that predates this; version 4 also
  carries the sqlstate field described below
- hardened the db2, informix, odbc, firebird and oracle connection
  modules' date/time inputBind() overload, which
  ignored its isnull argument and would bind the possibly
  stale date fields instead of sql null if a null date bind
  ever reached it - no code path today can actually deliver
  one, but the isnull indicator is now passed through to
  each backend's own null-bind mechanism (SQLBindParameter's
  null indicator for db2/informix/odbc, firebird's XSQLVAR
  sqlind/sqltype convention, and oci's indp for oracle),
  matching what each file's string-bind overload already did
- the firebird protocol module now describes bind parameter types from
  the backend's own prepare, so select, update and delete binds
  describe correctly, rather than only from an insert's column
  list
- the tds protocol module sends an envchange for the packet size it
  negotiates with a tds 5.0 client now, rather than renegotiating
  silently and then chunking at a size the client was never told
- the tds protocol module answers a failed tds 5.0 login with a login ack
  carrying the "failed" byte now, the way a real ase does
- the tds protocol module bounds how many commands one request may carry
  now - neither maxquerysize nor maxrequestsize covered this, so
  a 16mb request packed with minimal tds 5.0 language tokens or
  tds 7.x rpcs was millions of backend round trips, with the
  whole response held in memory
- the tds protocol module refuses just the one tds 5.0 command it can't
  answer now, and goes on to the commands behind it in the same
  buffer, wherever the token carries a length that lets the walk
  step over it - it gave up on the whole buffer before, so a
  ct-lib cursor, which sends declare, rows and open together,
  got a single error instead of one per command
- the tds protocol module strips the "create proc <id> as " wrapper a
  ct-lib client puts in front of a dynamic prepare - a real ase
  turns that into a stored procedure named for the id, which
  isn't portable to any other backend and would outlive the
  session that asked for it
- the tds protocol module's bulk load row loop documents why it isn't
  bounded by MAX_COMMANDS_PER_REQUEST the way the language-token
  and rpc loops are - it builds no growing response as it goes,
  and a legitimate bulk copy of a large table needs exactly as
  many backend round trips as it has rows, so an arbitrary row
  cap would break real use rather than stop abuse of it
- the tds protocol module honors a pre-tds7 client's declared
  integer/float byte order now, read from the login record's
  typeflags block, instead of always assuming little-endian
- the tds protocol module honors a pre-tds7 client's declared
  charset and language now, read from the login record,
  instead of always converting as utf-8 and ignoring both
  fields
- moved the FW_FIX_LIBTOOL call in configure.in ahead of AC_OUTPUT - the
  macro registers an AC_CONFIG_COMMANDS hook now, so calling it
  afterward dropped the libtool fixups from configure entirely
- the globaltemptables trigger now inserts "if not exists" into its
  create statement on postgresql - catching an "already exists"
  error there isn't enough on its own, since the error aborts
  the whole surrounding transaction before the trigger's recovery
  can take effect, and every later statement in that transaction
  is then refused until it's rolled back
- the tds protocol module grants the cursor and multi-row-fetch
  capability bits to a tds 5.0 client now - without the cursor
  bit ct-lib refuses every cursor command before it reaches the
  wire - while the positional scroll bits stay withheld, since
  the backend is forward-only
- hardened the tds protocol module's field() TDS_TYPE_CHAR/TDS_TYPE_VARCHAR
  case and login7()'s change-database/change-language path against
  the same unclamped-length-byte desync colName() and
  returnValueHeader() were fixed for - field()'s case is unreachable
  under today's type mapping and login7()'s cchdatabase/cchlanguage
  are already bounded by loginFieldFits(), but both now clamp to
  255 before the length byte and payload write the same way, in
  case either bound ever changes
- the tds protocol module's encrypted-login cipher lookup now searches
  the standard sybase/sap OCS install directories for
  libsybcomn64.so, the same way the sap connection module already
  does, instead of only one hard-coded ocs version under two roots
- the tds protocol module now refuses an "insert bulk" statement sent
  as a tds 5.0 language or dynamic sql command instead of
  running it on the backend, where it would have left the
  pooled connection in bulk-copy mode
- the tds protocol module now also refuses an "insert bulk" statement
  that arrives as the sql argument of an sp_executesql,
  sp_prepare, sp_prepexec, sp_prepexecrpc, sp_cursorprepare or
  sp_cursorprepexec rpc, the way it already refused one sent as
  a sql batch or a tds 5.0 language or dynamic sql command -
  running it on the backend would have left the pooled
  connection in bulk-copy mode; an rpc refusal answers with an
  rpc error instead of the done a batch or dynamic sql refusal
  uses, since rpc handlers don't own the closing done token
- oracle protocol module: deleted four stale FIXME comments claiming
  valid ttc codes were invalid, added the missing TTC_IO_VECTOR
  constant for one of them, and fixed putError() naming its ttc
  error code with TTI_EXECUTE, a constant from an unrelated table
- oracle protocol module: sendVersionResponse() now writes the version
  banner's length as a length-prefixed int, resolving a
  longstanding "writeLE?" comment over a writeHost() call that
  wrote it as a bare host-byte-order ub2 instead; and its
  trailing 4 bytes, previously a fixed, unexplained value, are
  now a packed version number derived from the banner's own
  version
- oracle protocol module: putColumnDefinition()'s legacy column
  definitions now derive their character/numeric marker bytes
  from the column's actual wire type, instead of always claiming
  character type
- oracle protocol module: close(), version() and open() now decode their
  request bodies instead of ignoring them - close() reads the
  sequence number and the wire's cursor id (the module's own id
  plus 1) and uses it to find the cursor to close, instead of
  trusting a session-wide hackcursorid left over from the last
  open/query/execute; version() reads the sequence number and,
  best-effort, the fields the wiki page's capture named after it;
  open() reads the sequence number, the cursor id pointer flag and
  the LPI opesiz instead of misreading the first two as a single
  "options" bitmask that was never on the wire; also fixed two
  debug labels ("requested version" in version() and "function
  code" in logonUnknown()) that were both actually the sequence
  number; sendOpenResponse() now sends that same plus-1 cursor id
  back to the client instead of a hardcoded 0, since close() needs
  a real id to read back
- oracle protocol module: sendFetchResponse() hard-coded its row count to
  1, so a legacy (TTI 0x05) fetch carried at most one row per
  round trip however many were available; it now sends every row
  left in the result set, bounded only by the client's negotiated
  packet size, since nothing after the fetch loop depends on the
  row count being exactly one - the client's requested row count
  itself is still not decoded, since where it sits in the legacy
  fetch request isn't known without a live capture, so this is a
  round-trip reduction rather than the real fix
- extended the tds protocol module's positioned update/delete key bind
  to decimal, float, and money primary key columns - only a
  whole-number key got the corrected non-string bind before, so
  ase's implicit-conversion refusal still hit those types;
  they're bound as a number now too, the same way money and
  decimal rpc parameters already were
- documented that errortranslation patterns regex rules (like the
  other patterns modules, filters and querytranslations, they
  share the same engine) compile without multiline/dot-all, so
  a pattern anchored with a trailing $ silently stops matching
  once a driver starts returning a multi-line message, as
  newer oracle clients now do by appending a "Help: ..." line
- brought the sqlr-listener, sqlr-connection, sqlr-scaler, sqlr-start,
  sqlr-status, sqlr-stop and sqlrsh man pages up to date with the
  -libexecdir and -bindir options
- oracle protocol module: pointed the source at the oracle wire protocol
  docs, marked the blobs that are still guesses, and brought its
  constant and function names in line with the docs
- oracle protocol module: stopped logging a piggybacked tti call as a
  bad ttc code
- the oracle protocol module's version response now honors the buffer
  length the client sends, rather than overrunning it
- the raise*Event() declarations and their sqlr-listener counterparts now
  carry a printf-format compiler attribute, so a non-literal or
  mismatched-arity format argument is caught at compile time; fixed
  a dozen call sites the resulting warnings turned up, including a
  latent size_t-as-int precision bug in debugHexDump()
- the oracle protocol module now returns an oracle error and keeps the
  session alive when a client sends a tti function it doesn't
  implement (or doesn't recognize), instead of dropping the
  connection
- the oracle protocol module no longer drops the session when a client
  sends a tns marker packet (eg. oci resetting the wire state
  after abandoning a fetch) - it answers with a marker of its own,
  flagged the way a real marker packet is, then completes the
  call the marker interrupted with ora-01013 before going back to
  waiting for the next tti function, instead of treating the
  marker as a framing error and ending the session
- the oracle protocol module now parses long-form (chunked) query text
  in a query3 request instead of ending the session - a client
  sends the query text as a run of raw length-byte-prefixed
  chunks (terminated by a zero-length chunk) once it's long
  enough, and the module reassembles them before handing the
  query off, the same as it always did for the ordinary,
  unchunked form
- the oracle protocol module now reports the affected row count in a
  query3 response's summary object, so an insert or a delete
  answers oci_attr_row_count with the rows it affected instead
  of 0 - a statement with no result set has no rows sent to
  report, and the count comes from the controller
- oracle protocol module: input bind variables work on the modern (10g
  and later) path now - the bind section of a query3 request is
  read rather than skipped, covering bind by name, bind by
  position, null binds, array binds and bind once/execute many,
  and a placeholder the client never bound is answered with
  ora-01008 instead of desyncing the session; added the
  re-execute call that a second execute of an already-bound
  statement arrives as, and moved both bind paths' values onto
  the cursor's bind pool, so nothing frees them individually
- oracle protocol module: rowid columns now describe as their real
  type and encode correctly on the wire, rather than falling
  back to varchar2 text - putRowData() packs the four numbers
  in a rowid's base 64 external form back into the binary form
  a live server sends; the oracle connection module now also
  recognizes oci's rowid type code 104 alongside the older 11,
  so getColumnType() no longer reports such a column unknown
- oracle protocol module: raw and long raw columns now describe as
  their real types and encode correctly on the wire instead of
  falling back to varchar2 text - putRowData() decodes the
  backend's hexadecimal text back into bytes, a raw as a plain
  length prefixed blob and a long raw in the long form; a long or
  a long raw column now also holds a query3 response's row back
  until the client names a long size, since a live server does the
  same and a row sent before oci defines its columns crashes it;
  and a null long or long raw is now the six bytes a live server
  sends for one instead of the single zero byte every other type's
  null is
- oracle protocol module: interval year to month and interval day to
  second columns now describe as their real types and encode
  correctly on the wire instead of falling back to varchar2 -
  putRowData() parses the backend's interval text and writes the
  fixed width binary form (5 bytes for a year to month, 11 for a
  day to second, each field biased) a live server sends; the oracle
  connection module reports both interval types as UNKNOWN like it
  does a timestamp, so the protocol module now recovers them from
  the backend's own size and precision instead of falling through
  to text
- oracle protocol module: long columns now describe as their real
  type and encode through the long form instead of falling back to
  varchar2 text - the "LONG" type name is ambiguous (oracle uses it
  for character data, sap/freetds for CS_LONG_TYPE, which is
  binary), so a column is only recognized as oracle's long when the
  real backend, per getNativeDbType(), actually is oracle
- oracle protocol module: timestamp and timestamp with time zone
  columns now describe as their real types and encode correctly on
  the wire instead of falling back to varchar2 - the fetched value
  already round-tripped through oci's implicit text conversion, but
  the describe metadata was wrong (type 1 and size 4000 instead of
  the real type and width); putRowData() now writes the real
  binary form (a date's 7 bytes plus 4 more for nanoseconds, and 2
  more again for a time zone's offset) a live server sends; the
  oracle connection module reports both as UNKNOWN like it does an
  interval, so getUnknownColumnType() now recovers all five of
  these types from the backend's own size and precision
- oracle connection module: recognizes a real timestamp/timestamp with
  local time zone column by its own oci type code instead of
  leaving both to be guessed at by size and precision - an oracle
  interval day to second column can legally declare a leading
  field precision of 0, which reads back identically to a plain
  timestamp's (size 11, precision 0), so the old guess
  misidentified one as the other and lost the interval's value;
  a timestamp(0) column, which describes narrower than a normal
  timestamp's binary form, is left alone as before
- oracle protocol module: putRowData() only encodes a real oracle
  timestamp, timestamp with time zone, or interval's binary form
  for a genuine oracle backend (per getNativeDbType(), the same
  check the long fix added) - db2, mysql, firebird, postgresql,
  sap, odbc and freetds all report their own native timestamp
  columns, and postgresql its own interval and timestamp with
  time zone columns, under the same type names a real oracle
  column would, and none of their text looks like oracle's; a
  column from one of those backends fronted through this module
  now falls back to null instead of risking a plausible-looking
  but wrong value
- oracle protocol module: clob, blob and bfile columns now describe as
  their real oracle types and hand out a real lob locator per
  row, and the lob operation call (tti function 0x60) is
  implemented for the read side - get length, read (single call
  and piecewise), file exists, open and close; the connection
  holds its position on the row a locator was handed out for
  until the client fetches past it, and a locator quoted after
  that is refused with ORA-22275 rather than read against
  whatever row is current.  every write side lob operation -
  write, trim, erase, create/free temporary - is read in full,
  along with the lob data the client sends behind it, and then
  refused with ORA-03001; before this every lob operation, read
  or write, fell into the generic unimplemented path, which
  doesn't read the request body at all and so desynced the
  session.  a bfile's directory alias and file name are the one
  gap: no server side call hands either of them over, and the
  client reads them out of the locator without asking, so
  OCILobFileGetName answers with two empty names rather than a
  made up pair
- oracle protocol module: output and in-out bind variables work on the
  modern (10g and later) path now - a placeholder in a pl/sql
  block is answered with the value the block left in it, so an
  output bind, an in-out bind and a bind the block sets to null
  all work.  the wire says nothing about which way a bind
  travels - an in-only, an out-only and an in-out descriptor are
  byte identical - so a placeholder in a block is declared
  in-out, which keeps a re-execute's request shape unchanged and
  leaves a block that only reads the value behaving as it always
  did.  the response carries the io vector (ttc 0x0b) that says
  which way each bind went, and then the values themselves (ttc
  0x07), each with a signed null indicator behind it; a
  re-execute answers with the values alone, since the client
  already learned the directions from the execute before it.
  the oracle connection module has no in-out bind of its own, so
  the values go through its output binds, whose buffers are
  pre-filled with what the wire sent - a plain bidirectional
  bind over a buffer this side owns behaves as in-out once
  what's in it at execute time is what the statement reads.
  dates, timestamps, intervals, raws and lobs stay input only
- oracle protocol module: ref cursor bind variables work on the modern
  (10g and later) path now - a block that opens a cursor for a
  select into an out parameter hands the client a cursor it can
  describe and fetch from.  a ref cursor's out bind slot isn't a
  value, it's the whole describe of the cursor the block opened
  followed by the id the client drives it with, and no null
  indicator behind it.  the module takes a cursor out of its own
  pool for each ref cursor bind, fetches from it once the
  block's execute has filled it in, and gives it back when the
  statement re-executes or closes - so a client that opens ref
  cursors in a loop doesn't run the pool dry.  the client drives
  the cursor it gets back with ordinary fetches on the same
  connection, which the existing fetch path already serves
- oracle protocol module: hardened putOutBindValues() against a ref
  cursor bind whose backing cursor is gone by the time the
  response is written - it now answers null instead of reading
  the bind value union as a string pointer
- the drivers report it through their own standard slots - the odbc
  driver through SQLGetDiagRec and SQLGetDiagField, jdbc
  through SQLException.getSQLState(), DBD::SQLRelay through
  DBI's State, php pdo through errorCode(), errorInfo() and
  the SQLSTATE[] prefix on its exception message, python db
  through a sqlstate attribute on the exception, and ado.net
  through a new SQLRelayException.SQLState
- the native protocol modules now send the backend's sqlstate instead of
  a hardcoded one - mysql, postgresql, tds and firebird - so a
  client speaking one database's wire protocol to a different
  backend gets that backend's sqlstate rather than a constant;
  this is a user-visible behavior change for anything that
  classified errors by the old fixed value, and each module
  falls back to what it sent before when the backend supplies
  nothing usable
- note that a connection-level error, and now its sqlstate, remain set
  server-side for the rest of the session - nothing on the
  query path clears connection-level error state, only
  cursor-level; the client apis behave differently, where
  clearError() clears the connection's error and sqlstate
  together
- error translation modules can rewrite the sqlstate now -
  sqlrerrortranslation::run() gained a sqlstate input and a
  translatedsqlstate output alongside the error number and
  error text it already translates, so a translation that
  makes one database's errors look like another's can make
  its sqlstate match too; this is a module abi change -
  third-party error translation modules have to be updated
  for the new signature and recompiled; the renumber module
  passes the sqlstate through untouched, since a sqlstate
  isn't an error number, and the patterns module rewrites
  it only when an optional sqlstate section of patterns is
  configured, since its main patterns are written against
  error prose
- oracle's own create-temp-table pattern now accepts an optional "if
  not exists" clause too, matching the same fix already applied
  to the shared base pattern; Oracle Database 23ai added "if not
  exists" to CREATE TABLE, including CREATE GLOBAL TEMPORARY
  TABLE, so a hand-written "create global temporary table if not
  exists ..." against a 23ai backend hit the identical misparse;
  firebird's own pattern is unchanged - firebird 5.0 does not
  have this clause, it's slated for firebird 6.0, which hasn't
  shipped yet
- dropTempTable() now logs a debugWrite() diagnostic when the
  drop query's prepare or execute fails, naming the table
  and the error, instead of discarding the result silently
  as before - the same silence that let a misparsed temp
  table name go unnoticed for a full release cycle; gated
  behind debug mode since a failed drop isn't always a bug
  (the backend may have already dropped the table, a
  rollback may have undone the create, etc.)
- truncateTempTable() now logs a debugWrite() diagnostic when the
  truncate query's prepare or execute fails, naming the
  query and the error, instead of discarding the result
  silently as before - the same gap already fixed in
  dropTempTable(); gated behind debug mode since a failed
  truncate isn't always a bug (the table may already be
  gone, a rollback may have undone the create, etc.)
- the oracle protocol module now advertises big chunk clr framing
  (CCAP_TTC3 bit 0x20) and negotiates it per session: a client
  that advertises the bit too gets each long clr chunk's length
  as a count prefixed ub4 rather than a single raw byte capped
  at 255, which is what the python-oracledb and node-oracledb
  thin drivers always assume and what a real 12.2 server sends;
  a client that doesn't keeps the raw byte framing.  a thin
  driver used to misframe every long value in both directions
  and fail with DPY-5003
- corrected the oracle protocol section of the config guide, which still
  said an oci client could never read a query's answer at either
  serverversion setting; that was fixed for serverversion=11.2
  by an earlier fix to the module, and the doc never caught up
- documented that ojdbc uppercases an unquoted username before
  sending it, and that the oracle_userlist auth module compares
  usernames case-sensitively, so a username ojdbc clients will use
  must be configured in the case ojdbc sends it, or configured
  twice
- the oracle protocol module sends a pre-10g OCI7 client under
  verifiertype="9i" the real 10.2 server's own fdo block in its
  TTC 01 protocol negotiation response now. part 1 of that block
  is 36 bytes there rather than the 31 the module sent, which
  moves the character set ids the client looks for behind it.
  part 2, the block size, the character set ids and the trailer
  were already right
- untracked doc/admin/installingpkg.wt, a generated file, and fixed the
  stale distclean path for it
- renamed a local sockaddr_un variable in sqlrlistener.cpp from sun,
  which is a predefined macro on solaris and expands to 1,
  breaking the build there
- cast the width argument of every "%.*s" (and one pid_t "%d") to int
  at the call site - the * conversion takes an int, and passing
  a uint32_t is undefined behavior that only worked because int
  and uint32_t are passed identically on the hosts that build;
  gcc 2.95 on solaris rejects it under -Werror
- the oracle protocol module answers a verifiertype="9i" client's data
  type negotiation from a real 10.2 server's own catalog now,
  rather than echoing the client's list back. it emits the
  server's types in the server's own order, declines BINARY_FLOAT
  and BINARY_DOUBLE the way a 10.2 server does, and lets the
  client's offer pick only the representation
- the oracle protocol module hands a verifiertype="9i" client cursor ids
  starting at 2 now, rather than 1 - which is where a real 10.2
  server starts, in every capture on file
- the oracle protocol module answers a legacy oci7 fetch in the encoding
  the session negotiated now. it was sending a portable-mode
  client the native-mode row header, per-column trailer and
  summary object, and an 8i era footer that a real server never
  sends at all
- the oracle protocol module renders a number column's value as digits
  rather than oracle's internal base-100 form when answering a
  legacy oci7 fetch, which is what a real server sends a client
  that defined the column as a string
- the oracle protocol module reads a legacy oci7 fetch request's cursor
  id and row count off the wire now, rather than taking the
  cursor from stored state and ignoring the row count. it no
  longer sends more rows than the client has room for
- the oracle protocol module sends the call status behind a cursor close
  response's status message now, the way a real server does. an
  oci7 client hung forever on the two bytes that were missing,
  and never went on to its logoff
- the oracle protocol module leaves the end-to-end sequence number off a
  verifiertype="9i" client's logoff response, and reads a close
  request's cursor id in the encoding the session negotiated
- documented the oracle protocol module's verifiertype="9i" option and
  the separate back-end an oci7 listener needs, in the
  configuration guide and reference; added a matching example
  config
- cast the width argument of 6 "%.*s" debugPrintf() calls in the odbc
  api to int - the same undefined behavior #9675 fixed in
  sqlrclient.cpp, with widths passed as uint32_t, size_t or
  SQLLEN instead of int
- the oracle protocol module now clamps the one-byte precision field
  it sends instead of letting a non-oracle backend's larger
  value (eg. router fronting a wide varchar) wrap silently into
  a small or negative-looking byte on the wire
- documented why no real client selects the oracle protocol module's
  native encoding, and why a 9i login never can
- the oracle protocol module honors an oci7 client's odefin's now - a
  legacy fetch sends back the columns the client defined and no
  others, the way a real server does, instead of every column of
  the select list; sending the extra columns overran the buffers
  the client had set up and segfaulted it mid-fetch
- the oracle protocol module's legacy fetch path now keeps a running row
  count, so the rows-processed field a fetch answers with counts
  up across successive fetches the way a real server's does
  instead of repeating each call's own count; a parse or an
  execute starts the count over, the way it starts a new result
  set
- oracle protocol module: generalized the oci7 statement-level error
  sender (formerly sendVariableNotInSelectListError(), hardcoded
  to ORA-01007) into sendOci7StatementError(), which takes the
  ORA number and message text as parameters, so upcoming tickets
  can send other oci7 errors like ORA-01008 and ORA-03001
  correctly; #9987 itself is a pure rename/generalization - every
  call site still sends the exact same ORA-01007 it sent before
- sqlr-scaler now restores the pool to the configured number of
  connections when connections die, rather than only growing the
  pool in response to client queue pressure
- the listener's dynamic-scaling minimum for listenertimeout now applies
  at the default listenertimeout of 0 too, rather than only when
  a nonzero timeout was already configured, so a client on such an
  instance now gets a clear error after around 110 seconds instead
  of queueing forever, whether the pool is merely busy or empty
- the listener can now time out waiting for an available connection
  even on a platform with no timed semaphore operations, as long
  as the wait is happening in a thread, by polling instead - this
  used to hang indefinitely, so a client pinging an instance whose
  connection pool was empty would block forever with no error
- hardened getColumnTypeName() and the oracle protocol module's debug
  logging around a sqlr-connection process on solaris8sparc
  found spinning indefinitely at 98-99% cpu during debug
  logging: sqlrservercursor::getColumnTypeName() now bounds
  its type code against the datatype list instead of indexing
  off the end of it, and cacheColumnDefinitions()'s debug
  logging no longer hands a possibly-NULL column type name to
  a %s conversion. the actual root cause turned out to be a
  va_list reuse bug in rudiments' charstring::printf() (see
  rudiments' own ChangeLog) - these are defensive hardening
  for the same code path (#10021)

### Removed
- oracle protocol module: deleted cancel() (no wire input can reach it)
  and the PACKET_MARKER constant (unused), the four
  putTti1Response() through putTti4Response() stubs and their
  unreachable switch arms (sendTtiResponse() never selects a
  version below TTI_VERSION_MIN), and the always-false query3
  branch of putColumnDefinition() (its only caller always passes
  false)
- removed dead code from the oracle protocol module: the fetch
  response's column-definition branch, which nothing ever took,
  the helpers that only served it, and the stored cursor id that
  nothing reads any more
- removed the oracle protocol module's pendingrow member and its
  nine now-inert clear() calls, dead since the query3 and
  fetch response builders stopped deferring rows (#10030)
  (#10056) (#10058)

### Fixed
- fixed several make install bugs (missing tds protocol module, doc/ on a
  host without wt, launchd/config.status chmod fixups)
- fixed a pile of sqlrsh bugs (csv output, bind variable commands, connect
  and response timeouts, -id password encryption handling)
- fixed the postgresql_connectstrings auth module accepting a one-way
  password encryption under cleartext passwords
- fixed sqlr-import/sqlr-export/sqlr-stop cli bugs (dropped option values,
  -krbservice, -table, missing exit codes)
- fixed csv export quoting and embedded nulls in the c++ api
- fixed sqlrimportxml/sqlrimportcsv segfaulting on a missing or empty file
  name
- fixed the c# data reader misreading almost every column as a byte array
- fixed numerous jdbc result set and result set metadata bugs
- fixed the odbc driver misreporting SHORT/TINY columns
- fixed timeout rounding in the client apis (c client macros, php pdo)
- fixed the freetds connection module's rpc path, character sizing, and a
  null char bind bug
- fixed a sqlr-connection cpu spin after SIGTERM against a dead database
- fixed a dangling pointer in the list api, a DATETIMEOFFSET disagreement,
  and stale postgresql array type spellings in datatypes.h
- fixed sqlr-listener blaming permissions for an oversized unix socket
  name
- fixed teradata protocol module connect-message decryption and a growing
  bind pool
- fixed the reformatdatetime translation's dateformat/timeformat fallback
- fixed the replay trigger overwriting/leaking logged bind variables
- sap/freetds connection modules: fixed exec/execute procedure parameter
  parsing and unrecognized lang= reporting
- fixed sqlrprotocol::readLenEncInt()'s broken switch/endian handling
- fixed the router pseudo-backend's outputBind() byte growth bug
- fixed jdbc maxfieldsize truncation and an isBinaryTypeChar/Int
  disagreement on postgresql bytea
- fixed a duplicate SQLR_ERROR_BULKLOADJOIN_IPC_KEY error number
- fixed odbc/jdbc/c# recognition of postgresql's underscore-prefixed array
  types
- fixed the mysql protocol module's negotiateMoreData() infinite loop,
  packet size clamping, user-defined-variable bind rewriting, and
  several integer underflow/oversized allocation bugs; fixed
  mysql_info reporting
- fixed assorted config guide doc typos and broken anchors
- sap connection module: fixed RETURN value/output parameter handling,
  cursor bind styles, image column corruption, binary bind
  character set errors, result set buffering/ordering (added
  maxbufferedrows), datetime output params, and a
  stuck-prepared-cursor bug
- tds protocol module: fixed cursor/result-set leaks, sp_execute extra
  parameter handling, DONE_ERROR reporting, longbinary rpc
  parameters, missing return status, and numeric/decimal output
  parameter precision
- fixed sqlrservercursor::getError() returning the connection's error
  instead of its own
- fixed the firebird protocol module's keepReading() debugging aid
- fixed jdbc result set metadata NullPointerExceptions and
  getColumnClassName()'s null return
- fixed an off-by-one in the c# api's column index validation
- firebird connection module: fixed SQL_LONG/SQL_SHORT/NUMERIC/DECIMAL
  fetch and metadata bugs, and added firebird 4 INT128/DECFLOAT
  support
- fixed the c# api's GetOrdinal()/getColumnNames() null column name
  handling
- fixed maxerrorsize="0" producing an undersized error buffer, and a
  related oob access in the oracle connection module's getError()
- fixed jdbc's getColumnClassName() NullPointerException on an unmapped
  datatype
- fixed the firebird protocol module mishandling sign-extended lengths
  from older clients
- fixed jdbc's SQLRelayParameterMetaData.isSigned() answering true for an
  unbound parameter
- fixed getError() across every connection module and the server's cursor/
  controller wrappers over-reporting copied byte counts
- fixed csv/xml/table/json export and csv import truncating fields at an
  embedded null
- fixed make install missing the oracle protocol module and its auth
  modules
- fixed sqlrsh's "reexecute" command returning progressively garbled
  column names on repeated replay; a re-execute now resets the
  result-set-header state the same way a fresh prepare does
- fixed the sqlite connection module leaking a database handle, and the
  lock it held, when switching databases with an unfinalized
  statement, which made every later write fail with "database is
  locked"
- fixed the oracle protocol module's describe-info message for oci
  clients, which read the block in front of it and a negative
  column scale in the count prefixed form rather than the thin
  drivers' form; an oci client could log in but never read a
  query answer, reporting ORA-03113 instead
- fixed the freetds connection module running asehostname(), a sap ase
  builtin, against ms sql server; it now probes the db version
  during login instead of waiting for the first cursor open, so
  it knows which backend it's talking to in time to pick the
  right host name query, and getDbIpAddress() resolves correctly
  on mssql as a result
- fixed the freetds connection module returning a bufferful of nulls
  instead of a zero-length field for an empty (but not null)
  text column on ms sql server; freetds doesn't null-terminate
  a zero-length value, so trimming the terminator underflowed
- fixed the freetds connection module reporting sap ase's database
  features (identifier length limits, max row size, schema
  terminology, sql keywords, isolation levels, and so on)
  for ms sql server backends too; it now reports the
  correct values for each backend, patched up during login
  once the db version probe determines which backend it is
- fixed the odbc connection module returning only half of a string
  output bind value; in unicode mode it bound the caller's utf-8
  buffer directly for the driver to write ucs-2 into, which capped
  the result at buffersize/2-1 characters.  it now binds a scratch
  buffer of twice the size, like the input-output bind path already
  did, and converts back out of it after execute
- fixed a double free in the c++ client api's DATE output bind reuse;
  defineOutputBindGeneric() didn't clear the bind's old timezone
  pointer when reusing a slot, so a failed execute after a prior
  successful DATE bind could free it twice - affects every
  client api and backend, not just odbc
- fixed the odbc driver smashing the caller's buffer in
  SQLGetInfo(SQL_FILE_USAGE); it wrote 4 bytes into what the odbc
  spec defines as a 2-byte SQLUSMALLINT, clobbering whatever the
  caller had next to it, which typically surfaced much later as a
  segfault in SQLDisconnect.  SQL_POS_OPERATIONS had the mirror
  bug, writing only 2 bytes of a 4-byte bitmask
- fixed eight null-handle checks in the odbc driver that dereferenced the
  connection handle while testing whether it was null; the driver
  manager rejects a null handle before the driver sees it, so this
  only reached code linking libsqlrodbc directly
- fixed the odbc connection module returning binary, varbinary and,
  on ms sql server, image column values from a plain select as
  uppercase hex text at twice their length; the fetch bind asked
  the driver for SQL_C_CHAR for those columns, and now binds them
  as SQL_C_BINARY instead, so raw bytes come back
- fixed the odbc connection module leaving a transaction open on the
  pooled connection; a transaction that a begin query started was
  ended with SQLEndTran, which returns success but does nothing at
  all while the driver is in autocommit mode, so commit and
  rollback both reported success while the transaction stayed
  open and kept its locks; it now ends such a transaction with a
  commit or rollback query, and only uses SQLEndTran for a
  transaction the driver itself started when autocommit was
  turned off; teradata is left ending its transactions with
  SQLEndTran, since it ends a BT with ET rather than with a
  commit, but the rest of the change applies to it too
- fixed the odbc connection module nesting a transaction inside the
  one the driver opens when autocommit is off; a begin is now a
  no-op there, since neither SQLEndTran nor a commit query
  commits anything at an inner nesting level on ms sql server
- fixed the server leaving a db in manual-commit mode after ending a
  transaction in the explicit and explicit-error transaction
  models; it turned its own autocommit flag back on without
  telling a db that has an autocommit of its own, so every
  statement after that ran in a transaction that nothing ever
  ended, and the connection went back into the pool holding its
  locks; affects the odbc and router connection modules, and the
  mysql module when a client selects one of those two models
- fixed the odbc connection module returning a bad column list on ms sql
  server; numeric_precision and column_key both came back as
  "0", extra came back empty even for an identity column, and
  data_type reported the identity flag as part of the type, as
  "int identity".  the microsoft driver's SQLColumns() returns
  29 columns rather than the standard 18, appending eleven
  proprietary SS_* columns after IS_NULLABLE, so reading the
  result by position landed numeric_precision and column_key on
  SS_IS_SPARSE and SS_IS_COLUMN_SET, which both read "0"; the
  module now builds an information_schema query for the column
  list on ms sql server, the way the freetds module already
  does, so data_type now reports the base type instead; every
  other odbc backend still uses SQLColumns, and no other odbc
  catalog call changed; note that within this one call the odbc
  and jdbc column list formats give up what SQLColumns supplied
  - data_type, sql_data_type and sql_datetime_sub come back
  null, column_size and buffer_length come back null for a
  numeric column, and a column list for a catalog other than
  the current one comes back empty - which is the same trade
  the freetds module already makes on ms sql server
- fixed the server not finding a table's primary key or identity column
  on odbc plus ms sql server; getColumnsInTable() looks for
  "PRI" in column_key and "auto_increment" in extra, and the
  column list came back empty there, so the upsert, replay and
  split-multi-insert triggers and the firebird protocol
  module's insert parsing all ran without them
- fixed the odbc module leaking output/inout bind buffers when a stored
  procedure had input parameters positioned before an output
  parameter; closeResultSet's delete loops now iterate the full
  bind count instead of just the output-bind count
- fixed the freetds and odbc modules not reporting auto_increment for an
  identity column on a local temp table on ms sql server;
  columnproperty() can't read a tempdb object across databases,
  so the temp-table column list now checks tempdb.sys.columns
  instead
- fixed the jdbc procedure parameter list format reporting a driver
  artifact instead of specific_name on odbc plus ms sql server;
  the microsoft driver's SQLProcedureColumns() returns 28
  columns rather than the standard 19, appending nine
  proprietary SS_* columns after the standard set, so reading
  the result by position landed specific_name on
  SS_TYPE_CATALOG_NAME; the odbc module now builds an
  information_schema.parameters query for the procedure
  parameter list on ms sql server, the way it already does for
  the column list and the freetds module already does here too;
  note that within this one call the odbc and jdbc formats give
  up what SQLProcedureColumns supplied - data_type comes
  back empty and sql_data_type, sql_datetime_sub and
  buffer_length come back null, the procedure's return value is
  no longer reported as a synthetic extra parameter row, and a
  parameter list for a catalog other than the current one comes
  back empty - which is the same trade freetds already makes on
  ms sql server
- fixed the odbc driver's SQLGetInfo null-handle guard; it rejected a
  null handle only for eight info types, and let a null handle
  through to the switch for the ~130 other info types that need
  a connection, where most arms dereference it and crash
- fixed six SQLGetInfo info types (SQL_DRIVER_HDBC/HENV/HSTMT/HLIB/
  HDESC and SQL_DTC_TRANSITION_COST) returning success without
  writing anything to the caller's buffer
- fixed a negative SQLGetInfo BufferLength reaching a size_t copy as a
  huge value
- fixed SQLAllocHandle's SQL_HANDLE_STMT error path writing the wrong
  null-handle constant with no null check on the output pointer
- fixed the freetds connection module's rpc output-bind fetch ignoring
  the null indicator, so a null output parameter came back as
  stale or uninitialized data instead of null
- fixed the freetds and odbc connection modules' ms sql server column
  list query leaving data_type, sql_data_type,
  sql_datetime_sub null and column_size/buffer_length null
  for non-character columns - which the odbc and jdbc list
  formats need but the mysql format doesn't ask for - and
  returning every column in the database for an empty table
  name instead of none; also added three-part catalog naming
  so a catalog other than the current one can be listed
- fixed the odbc connection module returning only about half of a
  wide (nvarchar/nchar) result-set column value; in unicode
  mode it bound the caller's utf-8 buffer directly for the
  driver to write ucs-2 into via SQLBindCol, which capped
  the column at maxfieldsize/2-1 characters.  it now binds
  a per-column scratch buffer of twice the size and
  converts back out of it after each fetch, the same way
  the output bind path was fixed
- fixed jdbc's DatabaseMetaData integer feature accessors throwing
  NumberFormatException when a connection module reports an empty
  value, as the odbc module does for getSQLStateType()
- fixed the odbc connection module getting "Invalid precision value"
  errors from sql server's driver when binding doubles, dates,
  times or timestamps.  it passed a column size of 0 for those
  types, and a fractional seconds scale that could exceed sql
  server's maximum of 7
- fixed the tcl api's inputBind reading the length from the value
  argument instead of the length argument when called with an
  explicit length, which passed a garbage length down and
  overran the buffer
- fixed the tds protocol module hex-decoding binary, varbinary and image
  column values from connection modules that return raw bytes
  rather than hex text, which halved and garbled every such
  value, and clamped those values' length prefixes to the sizes
  sent in the column metadata
- fixed the odbc connection module sizing its charset conversion buffer
  from a null-terminated length rather than the caller's byte
  count, which made any bind or query containing an embedded null
  fail with a bogus "iconvert::convert(): Argument list too long"
  server message
- fixed the tds protocol module parsing a uniqueidentifier parameter and
  throwing it away, which stored a null instead of the value the
  client sent
- fixed the tds protocol module reporting usertype 0x0050 for a datetime2
  column under tds 7.3 and up - 0x0050 marks a
  timestamp/rowversion column, not a date/time column, so a
  datetime2 column reports usertype 0 now, like every other
  ordinary column
- fixed the tds protocol module passing a fixed-length binary rpc
  parameter through with only the bytes that arrived - a
  binary(n) parameter is zero padded out to its declared
  size now, the way a real sql server does it, and the way
  the module already blank pads a char(n) parameter.
  varbinary(n) parameters are variable length and stay
  unpadded
- fixed the odbc connection module truncating a fetched field or an
  output/input-output bind value at an embedded null - the
  length of a value the driver wrote comes from the driver's
  indicator now, rather than from a scan for a null
  terminator
- fixed odbc connection module string input binds truncating a value at
  an embedded NUL - the bind length comes from the charset
  conversion now, and SQLBindParameter is given a real byte
  count rather than a NULL indicator, which made the driver
  treat the value as a null-terminated string
- fixed the custom_nw logger truncating the logged bind description at
  the first bind whose type it didn't handle, silently dropping
  that bind and every bind after it - a date bind was enough to
  trigger it; also escaped the timezone, which reached the log
  unescaped and could inject delimiters into it
- fixed the sqlite, db2, informix and oracle connection modules ignoring
  the null indicator on a blob or clob bind, which bound an empty
  lob instead of a null one
- fixed the router connection module dropping the null indicator on
  string, blob and clob binds, which sent an empty value to the
  next instance instead of a null one - an app that relied on a
  null string bind arriving as an empty string will see a null
  now, which a not-null column will reject
- fixed sqlrsh flattening "inputbindblob X is null" and "inputbindclob X
  is null" to a plain null bind, and reading an uninitialized
  value when printing one
- fixed the router connection module dropping the null indicator on a
  date bind, which sent a non-null date to the next instance
  instead of a null one
- fixed the tds protocol module's bulk-load path flattening a null
  binary/varbinary/image field to a plain null bind, which lost
  its lob-ness before it ever reached bulkBinary()
- fixed the mysql, postgresql and firebird protocol modules discarding
  a null bind parameter's blob/clob type before the connection
  module ever saw it, even though each module already had the
  type in hand at the null check - the mysql type array, the
  postgresql oid, and the firebird field descriptor,
  respectively
- fixed the firebird connection module creating, writing to and closing
  a blob for a null blob or clob bind before ever checking
  whether the bind was null - the column was already bound as
  null correctly, but the blob it created went unreferenced and
  had to be garbage collected
- fixed the odbc connection module re-describing a null bind's target
  column on every execute instead of once per prepared
  statement, by no longer resetting the per-position describe
  cache in closeResultSet(), which doesn't change what's
  prepared
- fixed the tds protocol module's rpc-parameter path flattening a null
  bigbinary/bigvarbin/image parameter to a plain null bind,
  which lost its lob-ness
- fixed the router connection module returning null instead of empty for
  an empty string/blob/clob read back through the router, by
  telling its client-side cursors to return nulls as nulls and
  testing the field pointer rather than the field length
- fixed the odbc connection module misdiagnosing a null bind aimed at a
  binary/varbinary/image column as non-binary when an earlier
  parameter position was already bound, by describing null-bind
  parameter types up front at prepare time, before any
  parameter is bound, rather than lazily
- fixed the tds protocol module treating a null binary/varbinary rpc
  parameter or bulk-load field as an empty value rather than as
  null, since a length of 0 is how those types say null
- fixed the tds protocol module's field() writing 0xFF instead of
  GEN_NULL (0x00) for a null byte-length CHAR/VARCHAR/BINARY/
  VARBINARY column - unreachable under today's type mapping,
  fixed for consistency with the rest of the switch
- fixed the tds protocol module treating a null byte-length char/varchar
  rpc parameter or bulk-load field as an empty value rather than
  as null, the same length-0-means-null gap already fixed above
  for binary/varbinary
- fixed the firebird protocol module handing out the same handle number
  to a statement, transaction and blob at once, which corrupted
  the client library's object table and surfaced as "invalid
  BLOB ID" (sqlcode -901) on later calls
- fixed the odbc connection module reporting an rpc or stored procedure
  call with no result set of its own as a bogus 24-column
  result set on ms sql server, which the driver's internal
  sp_describe_undeclared_parameters call leaves the statement's
  metadata pointing at after the up-front null-bind parameter
  describe added above, by asking for the column count again
  once the describes are done
- fixed the tds protocol module leaking the converted message, server name
  and procedure name strings on every info and error token, and
  the program name on every login ack
- fixed the globaltemptables trigger to materialize a table on any
  statement that references it, not just an insert - a delete,
  select or update against a not-yet-created table used to fail
- fixed the globaltemptables trigger forgetting that a table needed to
  be re-created on a pooled connection reused by a later client
  session - the trigger's created flag lives on the pooled
  connection and outlives any one session, but a database like
  postgresql scopes a temporary table to the session that
  created it, so the flag now clears at the end of each session
- fixed the tds protocol module answering a tds 5.0 request buffer with
  exactly one done no matter how many commands the buffer
  carried - ct-lib expects one done per command, so a
  multi-command buffer left it waiting for results that would
  never come
- fixed the tds protocol module always sending 0 (undefined) for the tds
  5.0 done token's transtate field - the field is curcmd in
  ms-tds but transtate in tds 5.0, and it now reports whether a
  transaction is open
- fixed the tds protocol module sending 0 for the transtate field of a
  tds 5.0 done-in-proc or done-proc token - the field is curcmd
  in ms-tds, and both tokens can now turn up in a tds 5.0 reply
- fixed the tds protocol module writing a tds 5.0 unitext output
  parameter's value as utf-8 while declaring it utf-16 in the
  paramfmt ahead of it; added the missing SYB5BIGDATETIME and
  SYB5BIGTIME entries to the TDS5_TYPE_* block
- fixed the tds protocol module's colName() and returnValueHeader()
  writing a column or output-parameter name behind a single
  length byte without clamping it first - a name of 256 units
  or longer desynchronized the rest of the result-set or
  return-value stream; both now truncate to 255 the way
  preTds7RowFmt() and appendInfoOrError() already do
- fixed the tds protocol module accepting sql batch, bulk load and rpc
  packets from a pre-tds7 session - those handlers parse and
  write ms-tds only, so the request was misparsed and the
  response was shaped for the wrong dialect; they now refuse
  the packet the way preTds7Normal() refuses a tds 7.x one
- fixed the tds protocol module's remoteProcedureCall() answering an
  empty or one-byte rpc request with an empty response packet,
  leaving the client waiting forever - it now answers with an
  error and a doneproc, the way preTds7Normal() already does for
  an empty tds 5.0 buffer
- fixed the tds protocol module's pre-tds7 capability() sending two
  hardcoded byte arrays as its request/response masks no
  matter what a client asked for; it now grants the
  intersection of the client's declared masks and what this
  module actually supports
- fixed end-of-session never reaching a before-prepare or after-prepare
  trigger - the trigger module list only fanned the event out to
  its before-execute and after-execute lists, so a trigger
  configured when="beforeprepare", like globaltemptables, never
  got to clean up
- fixed the normalize query translation defaulting slashescape to
  "yes" against every database - that's mysql/mariadb-specific
  syntax, so a literal ending in a backslash (eg. an
  escape='\' clause) never found its closing quote against any
  other database and silently swallowed the rest of the query;
  the default now depends on the connected database, and an
  explicit slashescape attribute still overrides it
- fixed end-of-transaction never reaching a before-prepare or
  after-prepare trigger - the same gap fixed for end-of-session
  in an earlier release, but for endTransaction()
- fixed sqlrservercontroller::endSession() leaving temp-table drops
  uncommitted in a transaction it re-opens but never closes,
  so the next session on that pooled connection could inherit
  an uncommitted drop from the one before it
- fixed the globaltemptables trigger not noticing a client rollback -
  a rollback undoes a "create temp table" issued earlier in the
  same transaction, but the trigger's "created" flag had no way
  to learn that, so the next reference to the table skipped the
  create and failed against a table that no longer existed
- fixed the tds protocol module binding a positioned update or
  delete's primary key value as a string - ms sql server
  converts that implicitly, so it never showed up on the
  sp_cursor path, but ase refuses it outright; only a
  whole-number key is converted, since a decimal or a float
  wouldn't survive the round trip
- fixed the tds protocol module honoring a trailing rpc batch flag with
  nothing behind it - the flag alone told remoteProcedureCall()'s
  batch loop another rpc was coming, so the batch's last done
  carried DONE_MORE instead of DONE_FINAL and a ct-lib client sat
  waiting for a result that would never arrive
- fixed the tds protocol module's colName() and returnValueHeader()
  mangling a multi-byte utf-8 column or output-parameter name -
  both widened it to ucs-2 one unit per source byte instead of
  decoding it, garbling any accented, cjk, or other multi-byte
  character; both now go through the same utf8ToUcs2() converter
  the row-data path already uses, clamping the character count
  it returns to 255 rather than the raw byte count; the rpc
  parameter name array that feeds returnValueHeader() also now
  stores the utf-8 byte length instead of the ucs-2 unit count
  read off the wire, so a multi-byte parameter name is no longer
  truncated on its way into that array
- fixed the tds protocol module sending ms-tds info/error tokens to a
  pre-tds7 (tds 5.0) client instead of tds 5.0's own eed token -
  the two shapes disagree on how the message number and severity
  are packed, which made ct-lib misreport both, and the ms-tds
  shape has no room at all for a sqlstate or transaction-state
  field; a client that asks to have eed suppressed still gets
  the old shape
- fixed the tds protocol module describing a not-null column wrong
  to a pre-tds7 (tds 5.0) client - it always sends a
  null-capable wire type for that column, so it now claims
  nullable regardless of the backend's own not-null
  constraint, matching what a real ase sends; ct-lib was
  rejecting the mismatched combination and leaving the
  whole column description blank
- fixed the tds protocol module's appendInfoOrError() mangling a
  multi-byte utf-8 info/error message, server name, or procedure
  name for a tds 7.x client - the same defect class fixed in
  colName() and returnValueHeader(), where writeVarchar() widened
  the string to ucs-2 one unit per source byte instead of
  decoding it; the strings are now converted through utf8ToUcs2()
  up front, with the token's precomputed size math (the name
  255-character clamp, the message-text truncation, and the
  token size itself) reworked to count ucs-2 units instead of
  utf-8 bytes to match
- fixed sqlrprotocol::writeLenPreInt(int32_t) writing a negative value
  as a length byte with its top bit set (0x81/0x82/0x84) followed
  by the magnitude - a form readLenPreInt() always rejected, so
  nothing could read what it wrote; a negative value is now
  written as two's complement in the existing 4-byte form instead,
  which readLenPreInt() already handles
- fixed sqlrprotocol::writeLenEncInt()'s two-byte and eight-byte forms
  writing raw host byte order instead of little-endian, so they
  only round-tripped with readLenEncInt() on a little-endian
  host; readLenEncInt() also gained an isnull out-param so
  callers can recognize putLobField()'s 0xfb null marker instead
  of it being misread as the literal value 251
- oracle protocol module: putField() wrote a CHAR/VARCHAR field's length
  byte as a truncating single-byte cast of its actual size while
  still writing the full, untruncated field data, desyncing the
  wire stream for values of 256 bytes or more; such values are
  now clamped to 255 bytes for both the length byte and the data
  written, as a safety fix - the correct wire encoding for
  longer legacy values is still unconfirmed
- oracle protocol module: sendFetchResponse() wrote the data flags word
  only inside its DEFINE block, so a legacy fetch that returned
  rows without DEFINE sent a type 6 packet with no data flags,
  shifting every field after it two bytes early; the word is now
  written unconditionally at the top of the function, and
  putError() no longer writes one of its own since its only
  caller now writes it first
- oracle protocol module: sendFetchResponse()'s exact-fetch trailer
  indexed two arrays using colcount-1 with no bound, underflowing
  on a 0-column cursor and reading past the end of both arrays on
  a cursor with 3 or more columns; it now only takes that path
  for 1- and 2-column cursors, the only counts it has captured
  wire data for, falling back to the existing non-exact-fetch
  trailer otherwise
- oracle protocol module: sendQueryError() and sendCursorNotOpenError()
  were stubs that sent nothing and returned false, ending the
  session on any query error or reference to an unknown cursor
  instead of reporting it; they now send a real ORA error - a
  summary object through putSummary() on a query3 session,
  putError()'s capture otherwise, with putError() now able to
  carry a caller-supplied ORA number instead of always 1403 -
  and the session continues; a backend error left with no usable
  number or message (0, out of range, or empty) falls back to a
  generic ORA-20000 rather than being sent as-is, since putSummary()
  reads an ora number of 0 as success; putError()'s message is
  now clamped to the wire's single-byte length field instead of
  desyncing the packet past 255 bytes; sendNotImplementedError(),
  an identical stub that nothing called, was removed
- sap connection module: a multi-statement tds 5.0 language batch (eg. a
  client's own two-select batch, or the metadata queries sap's
  blk_init sends as one language token) failed with ASE's "More
  than one SELECT statement is used to define the cursor" -
  prepareQuery() declared a cursor over any query starting with
  "select ", and ASE refuses to declare a cursor over more than
  one statement; a multi-statement batch now runs un-cursored
  instead, detected with a lightweight scan for a second
  top-level statement-leading keyword (skipping quoted strings,
  parenthesized subqueries, and a select joined by a set
  operator like union); also gave sapcursor a real
  nextResultSet(), so the un-cursored path (this new case, and
  the existing exec/execute/{call}/DML/DDL cases) can expose
  more than the first row-bearing result set of a query - fixes
  a latent buffer leak in the column-binding path along the way,
  hit every time nextResultSet() rebinds a further result set
- oracle protocol module: query(), query2() and execute() each
  discarded the client's cursor id and used one session-wide
  variable instead, so only one cursor could be open per session;
  they now honor the real id each request carries, using the
  "wire id is the controller's own id plus 1" convention
  query3()/close()/occa() already used; open() and query3() still
  record that variable, since two calls still need a fallback
  instead of a per-request id: the legacy Fetch request has no
  cursor id field on the wire at all, and execute() doesn't branch
  by session, so it misparses a query3 session's request using the
  legacy layout
- oracle protocol module: query2() hard-coded its bind count to 1 and its
  bind type to VARCHAR instead of reading either from the request,
  so a statement with more than one bind left the rest unparsed;
  it now derives the real count by counting bind markers in the
  prepared query text, since the Bind Value Request itself carries
  no count or per-value type on the wire; bindParameters() had a
  single live "assume varchar" arm below a large block of
  commented-out mysql code that never applied here (it decoded
  mysql's binary bind format against oracle's type codes) - the
  dead block is gone and real arms now group oracle's bind type
  codes by the right SQLRSERVERBINDVARTYPE (string, blob or
  clob) while still reading the same length-prefixed text every
  bind value arrives as, ready for when a real per-bind type
  list can be sourced - every bind is still presented as varchar
  today, since the request carries no type list yet; also fixed
  a bind value leak on a second bind against the same cursor and
  added bounds checks on the bind value header and size against
  the received packet
- oracle protocol module's legacy row path wrote a terminator after
  every non-null non-lob column regardless of whether putField()
  wrote a value for it, and 21 of putField()'s 22 column types
  were unimplemented stubs that wrote nothing at all, desyncing
  the row for any of those types (NUMBER being the commonest);
  putField() now reports whether it wrote a value and putRow()
  only writes the terminator when it did; implemented NUMBER,
  VARNUM, DATE and FIXED_CHAR, the four types worth encoding
  given what the module already had
- fixed a stale "writeLE?" comment on the byte order marker field in the
  oracle protocol module's sendAccept(); confirmed writeHost() is
  correct there - it's a self-reporting byte order marker the
  client uses to detect our endianness, not a value with a fixed
  wire endianness
- fixed the oracle protocol module's execute() sending a misparsed
  cursor id in the "cursor not open" error on a query3
  session - execute() doesn't branch by session when parsing,
  so a query3 session's request misparses under its legacy
  layout; it now reports the last touched cursor's wire id
  there instead
- fixed the firebird connection module's output date/timestamp bind
  storing caller pointers by call order but reading them back
  by sqlvar slot index, which wrote the value to the wrong
  output variable when date binds were registered out of
  slot order
- fixed the firebird connection module's output date/timestamp bind
  never writing back the caller's microsecond pointer; it's
  now stored alongside the other date fields and derived from
  the raw ISC_TIMESTAMP fraction, since isc_decode_timestamp()
  doesn't carry sub-second precision
- fixed the firebird connection module's input date/timestamp bind
  silently dropping the caller's microsecond value; it's now
  added into the raw ISC_TIMESTAMP after encoding, since
  isc_encode_timestamp() only encodes whole seconds
- fixed the oracle connection module's output date/timestamp bind
  never writing back the caller's microsecond pointer; it's
  now always written back as 0, since the OCIDate/SQLT_ODT
  type it binds has no sub-second component at all
- fixed the firebird, db2, informix and odbc connection modules'
  input date/timestamp binds propagating a caller-supplied
  negative year/month/day/hour/minute/second/microsecond
  into the encoded/native timestamp struct, wrapping to a
  huge value in the struct's unsigned fields (or, in
  firebird's case, corrupting isc_encode_timestamp()'s
  result); negative fields are now floored to 0 first, like
  the mysql connection module already did
- fixed the oracle connection module's input date/timestamp bind
  silently dropping the caller's microsecond value; since the
  OCIDate/SQLT_ODT type it used has no sub-second component
  at all, input date/timestamp binds now go through an
  OCIDateTime/SQLT_TIMESTAMP descriptor instead when the OCI
  client headers support it, passing the microsecond value to
  OCIDateTimeConstruct() as nanoseconds
- fixed sqlrserverbindvar not initializing a date bind's tz
  pointer, so a declaration-only date bind could carry a
  stale pointer into a bind-pool slot reused from a
  previous query; the constructor now clears it as a
  first-use backstop, and the teradata protocol's
  declaration-only date/time/datetime cases now clear it
  too, since that's the path that actually reuses a
  cursor's bind slots across queries
- fixed getInputOutputBind reading back an input/output date bind's
  time zone without a null terminator, causing a buffer
  overread into adjacent bind-pool memory on non-odbc backends
- fixed sqlrservercontroller::copyBind allocating an unzeroed byte
  and pointing a date bind's tz at it when the source tz was
  null, instead of leaving the copy's tz null too
- fixed nextResultSet, resumeResultSet and fetchFromBindCursor sending
  stale output/inputoutput bind values left over from the
  previous command on that cursor, out of a bind pool already
  cleared by then; covers string, lob and cursor output binds;
  the c++ client already discards its bind definitions before
  parsing these responses, so nothing is lost by no longer
  sending them
- fixed the teradata protocol module crashing on an unsolicited or
  out-of-sequence COPKIND_CONTINUE, which dereferenced a null
  request pointer; also fixed a COPKIND_START received before a
  prior one's sequence finished leaking the earlier request's
  cursor instead of releasing it
- fixed the tds protocol module leaving stale bind counts on a pooled
  cursor: cursorExecute now zeroes the input/output bind counts
  when sp_cursorexecute carries no parameters, matching its
  sibling rpcs, and bindParams now zeroes the inputoutput bind
  count too, matching the defensive zeroing used elsewhere in
  the module
- fixed the firebird protocol module leaving stale binds on a reused
  cursor: runPreparedQuery now clears the bind pool
  unconditionally instead of only when it was already empty
- fixed sqlrcursor::parseOutputBinds/parseInputOutputBinds in the
  c++ api growing pvt->_outbindvars/_inoutbindvars past what
  the client actually bound, if the server ever sent more
  output/inputoutput bind values than that; the extra slot's
  uninitialized pointer could then reach findVar's comparison
  or the array's own cleanup logic; bind values beyond the
  client's own bound count are now discarded instead of
  growing the array to match
- fixed the sqlrclient protocol module clearing its bind pool at
  the end of every command instead of immediately before
  refilling it, matching every other protocol module; the old
  timing left a cursor's bind counts pointing into freed pool
  memory between commands
- fixed the teradata protocol module's bulk-load COPKIND_START path
  leaking the earlier request's cursor too, when it ran while a
  prior COPKIND_START sequence was still in progress; the
  earlier fix to this same leak only covered the non-bulk-load
  path
- fixed sqlrcursor::parseOutputBinds in the c++ api printing an
  output DATE bind's microsecond field from _inoutbindvars
  instead of _outbindvars in its debug-print block; with debug
  logging on, this could read or grow a garbage slot in
  _inoutbindvars, the same hazard class hardened against
  elsewhere in this function
- fixed the teradata protocol module leaking sqlrprotocol_teradata's
  req if a client disconnected before its COPKIND_START sequence
  reached a COPKIND_CONTINUE that closed the result set; neither
  reInit nor the destructor released it; free, which both call,
  now runs the same close-result-set/release/delete cleanup used
  elsewhere in this file and clears req
- fixed sqlrcursor::nextResultSet() in the c++ api discarding bind
  variable definitions/values on every successful advance to a
  next result set; MS SQL/SAP output parameters, and any bound
  values in general, now remain readable/usable after the advance
- fixed sqlrcursor::parseOutputBinds() and parseInputOutputBinds() in
  the c++ api overwriting a bind variable's previous string or
  date-timezone buffer with a new allocation (or with NULL)
  without freeing it first; not reachable yet since bind values
  only get parsed once per definition today, but became a real
  leak risk once nextResultSet() started preserving bind
  definitions/values across advances
- fixed sqlrcursor::defineInputOutputBindGeneric() in the c++ api
  storing an input/output DATE bind's timezone argument as the
  caller's own pointer instead of duplicating it;
  deleteInputOutputBindVariables() and parseInputOutputBinds()
  delete[] that field unconditionally, so a DATE bind that was
  defined but never sent (send=false, via the opt-in
  validateBinds() checking) could wild-free memory this object
  never allocated, on cursor teardown, clearBinds(), or
  redefine; the field is now always a cursor-owned duplicate,
  freed on redefine like the string case, and the now-unneeded
  null-out that used to run after sending was removed
- fixed sqlrcursor::parseOutputBinds() and parseInputOutputBinds() in
  the c++ api overwriting a BLOB/CLOB output/input-output bind
  variable's previous lobval buffer with a new allocation
  without freeing it first; same root cause and fix as the
  string/date-timezone case above, for the lobval union member
- fixed sqlr-scaler --help not documenting -bindir, even though it
  genuinely accepts and uses it to spawn sqlr-connection
- fixed sqlr-start inverting the success/failure result when starting
  the default connection for a config with no connections
  explicitly defined
- fixed the mysql protocol module writing the ok packet's info field as a
  raw string instead of length-encoded, causing mysql/mariadb
  clients to misparse it
- fixed the c++ client api reusing a cursor id after aborting its result
  set, which handed the same server-side cursor to two cursors
  on the same connection and ran one's query out from under the
  other
- fixed the odbc driver buffering the whole result set on the first query
  a statement handle runs, which ran the backend cursor off the
  end of its rows and left a positioned update or delete against
  the cursor name with no current row
- fixed the sap connection module declaring every cursor read only,
  which made ase refuse a positioned update or delete against a
  client-named cursor
- fixed the firebird connection module leaving a cursor name claimed on
  a closed statement, which could make an unrelated statement's
  attempt to use the same name fail with "already has a cursor
  assigned" once that statement got reused by another session
- fixed make never building test/protocol/oracle or test/protocol/teradata,
  which made the oracleprotocol and teradataprotocol test suites
  fail before they even reached the module; their java clients
  still build only when a jdk is configured
- oracle protocol module: fixed an oci client hanging forever on
  OCIServerVersion - the version response's banner and packed
  version number were both a byte short of what a real server
  sends, and it was missing its trailing status message
- fixed the oracle protocol module's version response reporting 8.0.5
  instead of the version it reports at login
- fixed the oracle protocol module sending trailing bytes past a short
  statement's text to the backend on the query3 path - an oci
  client declares the query size as a buffer size rather than a
  byte count, and telling that apart from the byte count only by
  whether it fits in the packet doesn't work for a statement
  short enough that both fit, so "commit" came back ora-00911
- fixed the oracle protocol module's long-form (chunked) value framing on
  the way out to a client - each chunk's length was written as a
  length-prefixed integer, with the run terminated the same way,
  but a client reads a single raw length byte (255 bytes per
  chunk at most) and a single zero byte to end the run, unless
  big chunks have been negotiated, which the module doesn't
  advertise
- fixed the oracle protocol module's describe-only execute
  (OCI_DESCRIBE_ONLY) not executing internally, so it never
  reported column metadata and left clients believing the
  statement was never run; column metadata now reports real
  precision, scale and date typing instead of collapsing every
  type down to number/char/varchar2; fixed a stale column-type
  cache that could serve a describe's types to a later execute,
  a too-small described buffer size for a still-text-encoded
  column that could stall a client mid-fetch, a describe-only
  request that could run dml, and a two-digit date year with no
  century inference
- fixed the oracle connection module never checking OCIStmtFetch()'s
  return value, so a fetch-time backend error (eg. a row that
  fails to evaluate) was silently treated as end of data for
  every client api, not just the oracle protocol module; the
  oracle protocol module compounded this by reporting such an
  error, when it did reach it, as ora-01403 "no data found"
  instead of the real error
- fixed the oracle protocol module's sendFetch3Response() and
  sendQuery3Response() packing an array fetch or a prefetch into
  one packet with no bound on its size, so a wide enough batch
  grew past the negotiated packet size and desynced the client
  (later superseded by the fix further down this section, where
  both builders were changed again to split the response across
  packets instead of capping it)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) fetch response
  path ignoring a getField() failure and falling through to send
  the stale, untranslated field it left behind as though it were
  good; it now reports the error back to the client instead
- fixed the oracle protocol module's describe-only handling so it no
  longer re-executes a cursor that's already been executed - it
  now answers from the cursor's cached column metadata instead,
  so a describe issued while a result set is mid-fetch no longer
  rewinds it or leaves the row count stale
- fixed the oracle protocol module's modern (query3) fetch response
  path ignoring a getField() failure and sending the client a
  null value in its place; it now reports the error back to the
  client instead, same as the legacy fetch response path
- fixed the oracle protocol module's close and occa cursor-close
  handlers to also clear columntypescached and rowssent when
  releasing a cursor back to the pool, matching releaseRefCursors()
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) fetch response
  path desyncing the row stream - a null value wrote no bytes at
  all, a lob column skipped the row terminator, and a column
  whose internal type had no wire type fell through the field
  writer silently; it also no longer hands the field writer a
  NULL field pointer that getField() left behind
- fixed the oracle protocol module's close, occa, and releaseRefCursors
  cursor-close paths to also clear cursorbindcounts and the
  input-output bind count when releasing a cursor back to the
  pool, for consistency with the columntypescached/rowssent fix
  above; another latent asymmetry with no known live reproducer,
  found by inspection
- fixed sqlrtriggers' endSession()/endTransaction() calling a
  trigger twice when it spanned both a prepare phase and an
  execute phase (eg. when="all") - the plugin list only
  deduped within each phase pair (before/after-execute,
  before/after-prepare), not across them; both methods now
  walk all four lists with a full cross-dedup so each trigger
  fires exactly once
- fixed DBD::SQLRelay binding DBI's State attribute to
  $DBD::SQLRelay::state rather than $DBD::SQLRelay::sqlstate,
  which is the name DBI actually writes, so State never got
  populated at all
- fixed the php pdo driver's exec() reading the error off the connection
  when the query had run on a cursor, so it reported whatever
  the connection last saw instead of the failure that just
  happened
- fixed the mysql protocol module writing a short sqlstate into the ERR
  packet's fixed five-byte slot - two sites passed an empty
  one, which shifted the error message and left the client
  reading the rest of the packet at the wrong offset
- fixed the db2 and informix connection modules reading an uninitialized
  local back as a string when SQLGetDiagRec left it untouched
- fixed the patterns error translation module erasing the error text
  instead of passing it through unchanged when configured with no
  patterns
- fixed the php pdo driver's exec() leaving errorInfo() elements 1 and
  2 empty even after the previous fix - fetch_err read them off
  the connection, but exec()'s cursor was already gone by then,
  so the driver now stashes them on the connection handle while
  the cursor is still alive
- fixed checkForTempTable() misparsing the table name when a
  create-temp-table statement carries an "if not exists" clause -
  e.g. injected by the globaltemptables trigger on postgresql, or
  written directly on mysql/sqlite/db2 - which captured the
  literal word "if" as the table name and silently broke
  temp-table auto-drop at end-of-session
- fixed checkForTempTable() still misparsing the table name in two
  cases the previous fix missed: a mixed/title-case "if not
  exists" (or mixed-case "create"/"temp"/"table" generally),
  since the pattern only spelled out all-lowercase and
  all-uppercase alternatives; and a create statement with no
  space before the column list's opening paren, since the
  table-name walk stopped only at whitespace - both silently
  broke temp-table auto-drop the same way the previous bug did;
  the pattern (and the on-commit-preserve-rows pattern, which had
  the same gap) now compile case-insensitively, and the walk also
  stops at '('; the same walk fix was applied to oracle, firebird
  and db2's own separate copies of the same table-name walk
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) fetch response
  path rejecting a cursor with more than 255 columns instead of
  silently truncating the wire column count and desyncing the
  client's parse of every row that follows; also fixed
  putColumnDefinitions()'s column-size total wrapping instead of
  clamping for a wide row, and the packet-full check running
  before the very first row was even attempted, which could
  report a fetch as empty on a small negotiated sdu without
  trying to fetch anything
- fixed the oracle protocol module dropping bind values when a client
  executes at iterations 0; a thin-mode client can send a real
  TTC_ROW_DATA block while still claiming 0 iterations, and the old
  code's iterations-bounded read loop skipped it entirely, giving
  ORA-01008 on a plain bind and a silent NULL bind on a re-executed
  plsql block; the block count is now derived from what's left in
  the packet whenever iterations is claimed as 0, and the normal
  iterations-bounded path is unchanged
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) fetch path
  sending a lob column using a mysql-style length-encoded
  integer and a 0xfb null marker (borrowed wholesale from the
  mysql protocol module) instead of oracle's own clr framing;
  now uses the same length-byte/long-form-chunk/null-byte
  convention already used everywhere else in the file, with
  each chunk capped to the wire's per-chunk maximum the way
  the file's other long-form clr writers already are
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) fetch path:
  the per-column row terminator is a fixed 4-byte zero word after
  every column, not a 2-byte word gated to the last column and
  tied to the exact-fetch flag; the exact-fetch and plain-fetch
  trailers are a single, colcount-independent byte sequence
  instead of a lookup table capped at 2 columns.  confirmed
  against real Oracle9i OCI7 client captures for 1 through 5
  columns
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) fetch path to
  frame a lob column's inline bytes with the clr long-form
  marker unconditionally, rather than only above a 252-byte
  length threshold.  confirmed against real Oracle9i OCI7
  client captures that a 10-byte and a 30-byte lob both use
  the long form on the wire; also confirmed from the same
  captures that the legacy path really does send lob bytes
  inline rather than a locator, settling the question the
  putLobField() framing fix in the prior entry's ticket left
  open
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) fetch path
  to write a row header before the first row of a bare re-fetch
  on an already-described cursor: an outer TTC_ROW_HEADER ttc
  code followed by a fixed block whose 4th byte is the column
  count, both previously missing.  confirmed against real
  Oracle9i OCI7 client captures for 1 through 5 columns
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) query2 path to
  return row data when a request combines execute and fetch into
  one call, instead of always answering with a no-rows TTC_OK.
  confirmed against real Oracle9i OCI7 client captures for 1
  through 5 columns that the combined call gets the same row
  header, row data and trailer a separate legacy fetch call gets
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) query2 and
  fetch paths reading bits 8 and up of the request's "options"
  field as independent flags (commit-on-success, exact fetch,
  send iov); real Oracle9i OCI7 client captures of the same call
  repeated at different call-sequence positions show those bits
  are actually a per-call sequence counter echoed back into the
  field, not real flags - one capture even shows the counter's
  value coincidentally setting the commit-on-success bit on a
  plain execute with no commit due for several calls yet.
  query2() no longer commits off that bit, since every real
  commit in every capture arrives as its own separate call
  instead, and fetch() no longer reads exact-fetch or send-iov
  off it either, since a standalone legacy fetch is always the
  plain, non-exact case in every capture on file
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (pre-query3) query2 path's
  no-rows TTC_OK response, sent for a plain parse/bind/execute
  with no combined fetch: it was a decades-old guessed byte array,
  4 bytes short of and structurally different from the real
  trailer. confirmed against real Oracle9i OCI7 client captures
  for 1 through 5 columns that the real response reuses the same
  trailer shape the legacy fetch path already builds; the one
  opaque per-session field in it, with no correlation to anything
  else on the wire, is sent as zero rather than guessed
- fixed the oracle protocol module sending a pre-10g OCI7 client under
  verifiertype="9i" a datatype negotiation response the client
  can't parse, hanging or aborting the connection before login is
  ever attempted. the module's TTC 01 capability arrays are now
  the real 10.2 server's own bytes for such a client, and its
  TTC 02 answers the client's declared type catalog instead of
  skipping it. the module's banner never matches a client's, so
  every session runs in portable mode, where both sides exchange
  their full catalogs - a pre-10g client writes each field of
  that list as a ub1 rather than a ub2, and is now parsed and
  echoed back in that format
- fixed the oracle protocol module's O3LOGON challenge sending a 31 byte
  summary object where a real 10.2 server sends 94, leaving a
  pre-10g OCI7 client blocked in read() waiting for the rest and
  never answering with its password. the summary a server appends
  to that challenge is a fixed-width natively marshalled struct
  rather than the length-prefixed field stream putSummary()
  writes, so it is sent as a captured literal now, the way the
  authentication trailer already is
- corrected that O3LOGON challenge against a real 10.2 server's own
  portable-mode challenge to a pre-10g OCI7 client, captured by
  running the two through a banner-rewriting proxy so both ends
  chose the portable encoding the module always runs in. the
  session key's two byte length slot is a fixed width ub2 there,
  not the length-prefixed int the portable encoding uses
  elsewhere, and the summary object behind the key is 64 bytes
  rather than the 94 a native encoding session carries. the
  summary the module answers a successful O3LOGON login with is
  that same object now too, instead of putSummary()'s field
  stream
- fixed the oracle protocol module answering a verifiertype="9i"
  connection with its default AL32UTF8 charset instead of the
  single-byte charset (31) a real 10.2 server sends this
  client. AL32UTF8 is variable-width, which was the actual
  source of the 3x buffer length inflation seen in the
  client's datatype negotiation request, misattributed before
  to the ENCODING_CONV_LENGTH flag
- fixed the oracle protocol module's o3logon phase-1 challenge and
  login-success summary against a real portable-mode reference -
  a SPARC Solaris OCI7 client's login into an x86 linux 10.2
  server. the session key length slot follows the encoding
  rather than always being a fixed-width ub2, and the summary
  object is 29 bytes, not the 64 taken from a capture that
  turned out not to be portable-mode at all
- fixed the oracle protocol module appending an end-to-end sequence
  number to a verifiertype="9i" connection's tti version
  (0x3b) response. the field came after 9i - a real 10.2
  server answering a real OCI7 client ends that response at
  the call status - and the extra byte cost the client the
  whole call: it read the fields it knew, found one byte left
  over, sent a marker, cancelled (ORA-01013), and failed its
  next call with ORA-03120
- fixed the oracle protocol module's tti open (0x02) response. it
  wrote the cursor id, two 0x00 bytes and a bare TTC_STATUS
  code with nothing behind it. a real 10.2 server answering a
  real OCI7 client sends the cursor id, TTC_STATUS and the
  call status - the same status message a version response
  ends with - in both encodings, and a client that reads a
  truncated one sends a marker, cancels (ORA-01013), and fails
  its next call with ORA-03120
- fixed the oracle protocol module's tti query2 (0x47) request header.
  it read three raw big-endian ub2s - "options", "moreoptions"
  and a cursor id - which is the shape the wiki page's own
  table records. the header is really a one-byte call sequence
  number, then the options bitmask and the cursor id, each
  written as a count: a length-prefixed int in the portable
  encoding, a fixed four bytes little-endian in the native one,
  the same shape query3's request header uses. the old read
  landed on the right bytes only in the native encoding, and
  even there only by coincidence, so a real OCI7 client - which
  always talks to this module in the portable encoding - had
  its cursor id read as 0x0100 plus the real one and got
  ORA-01001, invalid cursor, and its options read out of the
  wrong field, so nothing would have executed even had the
  cursor been found. the field boundaries come from the native
  reference capture, where every field is a fixed four bytes
- fixed the oracle protocol module's tti query2 (0x47) response. it sent
  an 8.0/8i-era captured literal plus the generic footer in
  every session, which is only ever right for a native-encoding
  one. a portable session - the only kind this module actually
  negotiates - gets a much shorter answer: a three-field
  lead-in of a cursor id and two more counts, then the same
  summary object the osql7 parse response carries, with a
  success iteration count of 1 rather than 0. query2() also
  never picked the call sequence number up out of its own
  request, so the summary echoed whichever call ran before it
- fixed the oracle protocol module answering the tns connect with a
  protocol version older than the client asked for. the answer
  was rounded down to a release boundary, and only 12, 11 and 8
  were boundaries, so every client between 8 and 11 was told 8 -
  a 9i client asking for 0x0137 or 0x0138 got 0x0136, the 8.1.7
  version. it answers with the client's own version now, capped
  at 12, the way a real server does
- fixed the oracle protocol module answering a verifiertype="9i" client's
  multi-representation data types with a representation no real
  server picks. such a client offers the integer types 25 through
  33 in three representations - its platform's, then the type's
  own code, then the universal one - and the module echoed the
  last of them, which is always the universal one. a real server
  answers with the first it can write itself, so types 27, 32 and
  33 come back in the architecture-neutral representation the
  client asks for rather than the universal one
- fixed the oracle protocol module reading a request's pointer fields one
  byte wide no matter what the data type negotiation settled. a
  client answered a native representation for the pointer type
  writes its own pointer there instead - four raw bytes, in its
  own byte order - so the module read one byte of it and
  misparsed everything behind it. the width now comes from the
  representation the module answered and the byte order from the
  connect packet's "value of 1 in hardware" field, which is
  also read host-independently now. the tti version, open and
  osql7 requests are what this affects
- fixed the oracle protocol module telling a client two different server
  versions. a session with verifiertype="9i" already answers as a
  10.2 server everywhere else, but reported whatever the
  serverversion attribute said - 11.2 by default - in the tti
  version response, and answered the ano negotiation with the
  version the client sent rather than its own. it reports 10.2
  throughout now, and the ano frame carries the module's own
  version the way every real server capture on file does
- fixed the oracle protocol module's login-failure error packet, in both
  the native and portable branches, which had never been
  exercised against a real login failure before and didn't match
  either encoding a real 10.2 server sends
- fixed the oracle protocol module reading past a short tti negotiation
  packet into the previous packet's leftover bytes, and logging a
  client's own disconnect (a bare eof packet, or an explicit
  olog-time disconnect) as a broken authentication exchange
  instead of recognizing it as the client walking away
- fixed the oracle protocol module's answer to an osql7 parse request,
  which sent the portable-encoded summary object to native oci7
  clients too - added a native-mode encoding, verified byte-for-
  byte against a real native oci7 client's captured session
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy oci7 fetch response
  writing zero bytes for a column whose value it failed to
  encode - a still-unimplemented datatype (raw, long, long raw,
  rowid, timestamp, timestamp with time zone, the interval
  types, or a lob column the backend didn't flag as one), or a
  value an implemented datatype's encoder couldn't parse - and
  reporting the row as sent successfully anyway - it now reports
  the fetch as failed for that row instead of handing the client
  a row that is silently short one column
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy oci7 fetch path describing
  a result set, named type, ref, or timestamp with local time
  zone column as a plain varchar2 and sending its raw field
  value as text - it now describes each as its own type, so a
  fetch of one fails cleanly instead of handing the client
  whatever bytes the column held, misread as a string
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy oci7 execute request header
  read - it read its three fields with no bounds check, the
  same aliasing risk #9658 fixed in query2 - a short request
  could read past the packet end into whatever the reused
  buffer held from the previous read
- fixed the python api (CSQLRelay.cpp) to build against python 2.2,
  which predates the bool type, Py_True, Py_False and
  PyBool_FromLong (all added in python 2.3) and Py_RETURN_NONE
  (added in python 2.4)
- fixed a double free in sqlrconfigs::loadConfig() - a failed attempt to
  load a config module left the dynamiclib pointer dangling
  instead of NULL, so it got deleted again by the caller; also
  made "Couldn't find id" list which config url(s) were searched
- fixed the oracle protocol module to handle the oci7 client's automatic
  post-login nls-sync ALTER SESSION call (tti function 0x27,
  parse-execute) instead of rejecting it with ORA-03001, which
  made the client send a break and abort every login once its
  post-login handshake completed
- fixed the oracle protocol module's o3logon parsing to recognize a real
  session-key login's tagged AUTH_TERMINAL/AUTH_PROGRAM_NM/
  AUTH_MACHINE/AUTH_PID fields instead of assuming everything
  past the user name is a flat string list, which made every
  such login fail with ORA-03113
- fixed the oracle protocol module's o3logon session-key login so it
  actually completes: the session key challenge now answers in
  the tagged AUTH_SESSKEY shape a real client expects instead of
  a bare length-prefixed buffer no client accepts; phase two's
  AUTH_PASSWORD is now read as a tagged field among AUTH_TERMINAL/
  AUTH_PROGRAM_NM/AUTH_MACHINE/AUTH_PID/AUTH_ACL/AUTH_ALTER_SESSION
  instead of a bare string; and the chunked long-form encoding
  AUTH_ALTER_SESSION's oversized value uses is now parsed
  correctly, including tolerating a trailing nul a real client
  includes inside the last chunk's declared length
- fixed the oracle protocol module's classic login: its session key
  challenge was one length byte short of what a real client
  expects, so a client that didn't get a correct password on
  its first try never got as far as sending a real phase-two
  login attempt at all - it broke the exchange right after the
  challenge instead, which surfaced as ORA-03113 rather than a
  clean rejection; also made recvAuthenticationRequest() answer
  a break/reset marker a client sends mid-exchange instead of
  treating it as a framing error and dropping the connection
- fixed the oracle protocol module's classic login misreading the user
  name and password out of a real client's request: each
  non-empty string in the request's trailing string blob carries
  its own redundant one-byte length prefix that the parser
  didn't skip, so it read from the wrong offset and got
  fragments of neighboring fields instead - harmless for phase
  one, since nothing checks its result, but it made phase two's
  user name mismatch phase one's, which the module correctly
  (if unhelpfully) rejected as someone answering the wrong
  challenge
- fixed the oracle protocol module answering a client's mid-call marker
  (the same recovery signal a client sends to abandon a fetch it
  stopped reading, or a genuine user cancel) with the wrong summary
  shape for a classic-login or o3logon client: the older
  putError()/putGenericFooter() object, meant only for genuinely
  pre-9i clients, rather than the same putOci7Summary() object every
  other response of this client family gets; a real server's own
  answer to a genuine client-side cancel confirmed the shape and
  found the error number's field, previously an unconfirmed always-
  zero one
- fixed the oracle protocol module misreading TTI_EXECUTE's cursor id
  on every call after a session's first: the request header was
  still an unverified raw three-field big-endian guess, so a real
  client's length-prefixed cursor id came out 256-ish instead of
  the cursor actually in use, which failed as cursor id not found
  and cascaded into ORA-03106/ORA-03120/ORA-03114 for the rest of
  the session; a real client's request confirmed the header is a
  one-byte sequence number followed by length-prefixed cursor id,
  iteration count and options fields, the same shape query2()'s
  header already used; also made the call echo its own sequence
  number in the summary object, which it never had before
- fixed the oracle protocol module hanging on a client's first standalone
  fetch after explicitly describing a cursor's columns: a date
  column's value went out as 7 raw bytes with no length prefix,
  while every other column type on the same row is length-
  prefixed, so a real client read the date's own century byte as
  the next field's length and blocked waiting for a field that
  size that was never coming; a real server's own answer to a
  standalone fetch, already on file but previously misread as a
  different call, confirmed every field on this path is length-
  prefixed, matching how a date is already written on the
  newer row-data path
- fixed the oracle connection module freeing its session and
  transaction handles under the wrong handle type on several
  failed-login cleanup paths, then reusing and freeing the
  wrongly-typed handle again correctly - a type-confused free
  followed by a double free
- fixed sqlr-connection potentially crashing during its own exit() when
  it fails to start up (e.g. a database login attempt fails):
  some vendor client libraries leave process-global state
  corrupted after certain kinds of failed login, and exit()
  running that corrupted state (e.g. glibc's atexit chain)
  rather than the login failure itself was the actual crash;
  since sqlr-connection installs a crash handler that only sets
  a flag and returns, the faulting instruction re-executed
  forever instead of the process exiting, so a connection that
  failed to start up (e.g. due to an authentication protocol
  mismatch with the backend) could keep sqlr-connection from
  ever coming up; sqlr-connection now exits immediately, without
  running that cleanup, whenever it fails to initialize
- fixed the oracle connection module reading a described column's
  precision and scale one byte too narrow: oracle writes both
  as 2 bytes even for an implicit describe, though they're
  documented as 1-byte there, so oci was actually writing past
  the end of each single-byte field and into the next one -
  harmless on x86 only because the following field's own read
  immediately overwrote the damage, and because the low byte
  of the pair happens to be the one that lands in the field on
  a little-endian host anyway; on a big-endian host
  (solaris8sparc) the high byte landed there instead,
  reporting 0 for any column with a real precision or scale
- fixed the oracle protocol module's old-style TTI_QUERY (0x03) request
  parser reading the cursor id as a raw fixed-width field instead
  of the length-prefixed count it's actually sent as, and its
  response unconditionally sending a canned TTC_ERROR blob instead
  of a real success summary; together these broke every old-style
  parse call an oci7 client made through this module, including
  sqlplus's own post-login bootstrap query, with ORA-01001
  "invalid cursor"
- fixed the oracle protocol module's modern-style TTI_QUERY3 (0x5e)
  request parser reading eight fields that don't exist on an oci7
  client's wire, running past the real end of the fixed-field
  section and into the query text's own length byte, breaking
  every query3 call any oci7 client ever made through this
  module - including sqlplus's own third post-login bootstrap
  statement - with a truncated read before ever reaching the
  query text
- fixed the oracle protocol module's o3logon login parser requiring
  every byte of a short-form length-prefixed string to be
  printable, when a real 9i client counts a value's trailing
  NUL inside its own declared length; the parser then gave up
  on the whole request, so a session-key login whose
  AUTH_ALTER_SESSION text was short enough to avoid the
  chunked long form died with ORA-03113.  the short form
  allows a NUL as the last declared byte now, the way the
  chunked long form already did
- fixed the oracle protocol module's TTI_QUERY3 (0x5e) request parser
  misreading the query text for a universal-encoding
  (thin/pointersize=1) client: the eight fields removed to fix
  the over-read above are only absent for an oci7 native-encoding
  client, and the zero-skip loop that used to find the query
  text after them only worked by luck when their bytes happened
  to be all zero; a universal-encoding client's own trailer isn't,
  so the loop stopped seven bytes early and handed the backend
  the wrong statement text - confirmed against a real ojdbc
  session's live capture as well as this module's own raw-socket
  test client, both of which send that trailer
- fixed the oracle protocol module answering a legacy (pre-query3) client's
  error with bytes no client can parse: a fetch past the last row,
  a failed parse/execute/fetch, an invalid cursor, a cancelled
  call, an unimplemented function and the commit/rollback and lob
  acks all sent a fixed 48-byte block plus a 41-byte footer, the
  same bytes in either wire encoding, and the client abandoned the
  call and failed the next one ORA-03120 instead of reporting the
  real error - a fetch past the last row reported ORA-03120 where
  a real server reports ORA-01403.  they now send the ordinary
  summary object with the error number in it and the message
  behind it, reproduced byte for byte from real 10.2 server
  captures in both encodings
- fixed an oracle protocol module bug that broke every oci7 query run on
  a cursor whose wire id wasn't 2.  the first of the three fields
  that lead a query2 response is a constant, not the cursor id,
  but it had been decoded from captures that were all answering
  cursor id 2 - where a constant and the cursor id are
  indistinguishable - and was being sent as the cursor id ever
  since.  a real 10.2 server sends 2 there whatever cursor it is
  answering, verified at cursor ids 1, 2 and 3.  any other value
  made a real oci7 client read the response, reject it, and
  cancel the call with a marker, which surfaced to the client as
  ORA-03106.  it went unnoticed because a session's first cursor
  gets id 2 and no test had ever opened a second one.  an exact
  fetch's trailer led with the same field and had the same bug,
  though a client doesn't reject a fetch over it
- fixed sqlrservercontroller::newCursor()'s no-argument overload
  handing out an id that could later be reused by a pooled
  cursor as the pool grew; it now cycles through ids that a
  pooled cursor can never be assigned
- fixed the oracle protocol module's native-encoding fetch trailer
  sending a hardcoded cursor id of 1 instead of the real cursor's
  id, in sendFetchResponse()'s copy of putOci7SummaryNative()'s
  object; with only one cursor open the hardcoded value happened
  to be right, but a second cursor got the wrong one.  the
  module only ever negotiates the portable encoding today, so
  this path isn't reachable yet, but the value is written for
  real now for whenever it is (#9978)
- fixed stagemodules.sh silently staging nothing on hosts whose
  /bin/sh doesn't support "!" as a reserved word
  (solaris8sparc); it printed a false "staged" line for
  every module while copying none of them, so a -buildtree
  test run there was testing an empty stagedmodules/ (#9972)
- fixed sqlr-scaler waiting forever for a new connection to start up on
  platforms without timed semaphore operations, which left it
  unable to ever spawn another connection
- fixed the oracle protocol module's reInit() resetting only lob pins
  and define lists between sessions; the rest of its per-cursor
  state (cached column types, sent-row counts, pending fetch
  rows, saved binds, and ref-cursor counts) carried over from
  whatever the previous session's cursor left in it, since only
  close() reset the rest, and only for a cursor the client
  actually closed - a client that disconnected with cursors
  still open, or died mid-fetch, left it there for the next
  session that reused the same sqlr-connection process;
  reInit(), close() and occa() now share one per-cursor reset
  routine (#9980)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's query2() legacy execute never
  caching column definitions, so an odescr arriving between an
  oexec and the client's first ofen re-ran the statement,
  silently rewinding the result set the client was about to
  read (#9973)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's sendExecuteResponse() answering a
  bare execute (no pending column defines) with a hardcoded,
  undecoded canned blob carrying no cursor id or call number,
  instead of a real summary object, which a real oci7 client
  rejects with a marker-cancel and ora-01013 (#9981)
- fixed the oracle protocol module refusing an oci7 bind value wider
  than 252 bytes, which arrives in the chunked long form
  rather than behind a single length byte; the value is
  reassembled from its chunks now, and a length byte above
  252 that isn't a null or long form marker is refused
  outright instead of being read as a 255 byte value
  (#9985)
- fixed the oracle protocol module writing an outgoing data response as
  one tns packet with no cap, so a row too wide for the
  negotiated sdu (eg. a large inline lob) went out oversized and
  a real client dropped the connection with ora-12592;
  sendPacket() now splits an oversized data packet into several
  ordinary packets bounded by the sdu; also fixed the connect
  handshake echoing the client's requested sdu back verbatim
  instead of negotiating it (#9989)
- fixed the oracle protocol module refusing an oci7 client's bind of a
  date, along with every wire type outside number and character;
  a date bind now decodes the same seven-byte layout the module's
  modern oall8 bind path already handled (#9986)
- fixed the oracle protocol module refusing an oci7 pl/sql block's out
  and in-out bind variables with ora-01007; the module now sends
  the io vector such a bind block asks for, always reporting
  in-out (the wire can't distinguish out-only from in-out, and a
  real server's out-only single-packet answer isn't reachable
  here since the value has to come from the client first), reads
  the client's follow-up value packet, then executes and answers
  with the resulting out value; confirmed against a live capture
  of a real oci7 client's out, in-out and null-out pl/sql binds
  against a real oracle 10.2 server (#9984)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy oci7 fetch path leaving
  raw, long, long raw, rowid (the plain text form), timestamp,
  timestamp with time zone, timestamp with local time zone, the
  interval types, and a clob/blob/bfile column not flagged as a
  lob unimplemented; each now sends what a real oracle 10.2
  server does, confirmed against a live capture of a real oci7
  client fetching every type from that server; the real
  sqlt_rid binary rowid form stays unimplemented, since its
  wire layout has no documentation anywhere accessible (#9746)
- fixed the oracle connection module folding a timestamp with local
  time zone into the same generic datatype as a plain timestamp,
  which lost the distinction before the oracle protocol module
  ever saw the column; a live oci7 describe showed this made the
  module report it as an ordinary timestamp (wire type 180)
  instead of 231; added a TIMESTAMPLTZ_DATATYPE (datatypes.h) so
  it carries through with its own identity and describes/wire
  encodes correctly (#9704)
- fixed the mysql protocol module's getColumnType() reading past the
  end of mysqltypemap[] for any column type name added to the
  shared datatype list without a matching mysqltypemap[] entry,
  now guarded the same way the oracle protocol module's own
  getColumnType() already was (#9704)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy oci7 fetch path sending a
  rowid column's text form regardless of what the client's
  odefin() actually requested; the requested wire type is now
  kept per define position and honored for the one pair with
  real evidence - a column defined SQLT_RID now gets the packed
  binary form putRowidField() already builds for the modern
  fetch path, instead of the SQLT_STR text form every requested
  type used to get; the requested buffer size, and every other
  (natural type, requested type) pair, remain unhonored (#9974)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's native-encoding fetch trailer
  sending a hardcoded rows-processed count of 1 instead of the
  cursor's real running row count, the same way #9978 fixed the
  cursor id field in the same trailer. the module only ever
  negotiates the portable encoding today, so this path isn't
  reachable yet, but the value is written for real now for
  whenever it is (#9993)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's execute(), reexecute() and
  parseExecute() (the TTI_EXECUTE handlers for a bare re-execute
  of a cursor query2()/query3() already parsed, and the legacy
  parse-and-execute-in-one call) never caching column
  definitions after a successful execute, unlike query2()'s
  execute branch fixed in #9973; a describe() landing between
  one of these and the client's first fetch could still find
  the guard flag unset and re-run the statement, rewinding the
  result set (#10001)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (oci7) fetch path
  under-framing a char column the client defined SQLT_AFC:
  a real server blank-pads such a value out to the
  client's whole define buffer and sends it in the clr
  long form, not the short form the module always used to
  write regardless of the define. a plain varchar define
  is unaffected and still gets the short form up to 252
  bytes, same as before; also capped a define's buffer
  size, which the fix reads off the wire, so a forged one
  can't blow up the padding written for it (#10014)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (oci7) fetch path sending
  indicator 0 and return code 0 for a null column, for every
  type, rather than the indicator -1 and return code 1405
  (ORA-01405, "fetched column value is NULL") a real server
  sends; a client reading indicator 0 sees the column as not
  null. the module already got this right for one case, a
  null long or long raw fetched through the modern (query3)
  path, but never for the legacy path any type went through.
  lob columns are unaffected; what a real server sends for a
  null lob on this path is still unsettled (#10013)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (oci7) fetch path
  always sending a date column's 7 byte binary form,
  ignoring a client define that asked for text; a client
  that odefin's a date column SQLT_STR now gets the
  backend's own already-converted date text, the way a
  real server does, instead of raw binary in a text
  buffer. every other define, including no define at
  all, still gets the binary form as before (#10018)
- fixed debug="protocols" (and any other debug="yes") on an instance
  producing no debug output; the implicitly-added debug logger
  set stdout="yes" but never file="yes", and sqlr-connection,
  where protocol-level debug actually fires, daemonizes without
  redirecting stdout anywhere, so the output went nowhere. the
  implicit debug logger now enables file="yes" too, so debug
  output lands in the debug directory regardless (#10019)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (oci7) fetch path
  sending indicator 0 and return code 0 for a null lob
  column, the one case #10013 left unsettled; a live capture
  against a real oracle server now shows a null lob gets
  indicator -1 and return code 1405, same as every other null
  column. under native encoding it also gets no leading value
  byte at all, unlike every other type, which writes a single
  zero byte ahead of the indicator; a live portable-encoding
  session broke when that same byte was omitted there too, so
  portable keeps the byte, matching every other type, while
  native does not - a real capture backs the native behavior,
  portable rests on live verification against the shape every
  other null column already used there, not a capture of its
  own. putLobField() now reports back whether it wrote a genuine
  null, so putRow() can send the right indicator/return-code
  pair; a real, non-null zero-length lob is unaffected (#10006)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (oci7) describe path
  remapping a rowid column's type to 104 (SQLT_RDD) instead of
  sending the internal type 11 a real oracle server uses; real
  oci7 clients on redhat9x86 and solaris8sparc both report a
  rowid column back as type 11, size 16 once the wire carries
  11 unmodified, matching a real server exactly (#10016)
- fixed the oracle protocol module handing a ref cursor back to the pool
  only partly reset, so the next statement to get that cursor id
  could drop columns or send a stale bind count - the stale
  defines and stale query2 bind counts are cleared now.  ref
  cursors opened by a ref cursor are also released now, rather
  than leaving a stale list behind that a later close could
  follow and steal live cursors from unrelated statements
  (#10000)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's query2 (oci7) pl/sql in-out
  bind path sizing its output buffer off a fixed 128-byte
  floor instead of the client's own declared buffer size,
  which getQuery2Descriptors() reads off the wire but never
  kept - a block writing back more than that into a bind
  given a short input value got ORA-06502 where a real oracle
  server would not.  the buffer size is now persisted per
  cursor the same way the query3 path already keeps its own,
  and installQuery2OutBind() uses it the same way
  installQuery3Binds() does (#10012)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (oci7) lob chunked-read
  loop (putLobField()) advancing its offset by the count
  requested instead of the count actually read, which could
  skip lob bytes on a short non-final read from the backend;
  not reachable given this backend's current buffer sizing,
  but fixed defensively for correctness and to guard against
  future changes (#10025)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy (oci7) execute sending
  ORA-01007 where a real oracle server sends ORA-01008 when the
  client's bind values run out with placeholders still unfilled,
  which is what an oci7 client binding a name the statement never
  declared looks like on the wire; other bad reads of the value
  block still get ORA-01007 (#10026)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's recvPacket() never reassembling an
  inbound request that spans more than one tns packet, the
  inbound mirror of #9989's outbound fix; a query or bind
  request too large for the negotiated sdu (eg. a chunked long
  form bind value) now has its packets reassembled on demand, at
  the point a parse actually runs out of bytes, since tns has no
  continuation marker to reassemble on otherwise.  originally
  scoped to the query/bind request path with query3's own
  request parse tracked separately; query3 (its request header,
  bind descriptor walk, and query text, including its chunked
  long form) is now covered too (#10036).  a few narrow
  points inside the covered path deliberately don't refill - a
  bind value boundary, a row data block boundary, and a
  descriptor header with no descriptor block behind it - since
  running out of bytes there is ordinary client behavior rather
  than truncation, and waiting for a packet the client already
  finished sending would hang the session (#10004)
- fixed the same lob chunked-read offset bug found by #10025 in the
  postgresql and mysql protocol modules' buildLobField() and in
  the native sqlrclient protocol module's
  getLobOutputBindSegment() and getLobFieldSegment(): each
  advanced its offset by the count requested instead of the
  count actually read.  unlike #10025's oracle case, this is
  reachable - a wire-protocol frontend isn't tied to any one
  connection-module backend, and both the db2 and odbc/informix
  backends genuinely act on a wrong offset (db2 skips lob bytes,
  odbc/informix truncate the read early).  fixed the same way as
  #10025, advancing by the actual count read and clamping
  against the buffer size (#10029)
- fixed the router pseudo-backend's getLobOutputBindSegment(): it
  compared the offset against the lob's length as a 32-bit
  value, which could underflow, and it always copied from the
  start of the lob instead of from the requested offset, so a
  second or later chunk of a multi-chunk read just repeated the
  first chunk (#10037)
- fixed a deadlock in the oracle protocol module's auth path: a
  modern client retrying a failed login on the same connection
  sends its own TNS marker after reading the error, and the
  module answered it with just a reset before waiting for more,
  which left both sides blocked in read() forever.  the module
  now runs the pre-error break/reset marker exchange for every
  verifier type, not just o3logon, and answers the client's
  later marker with sendMarkerCancelError(), matching the
  pattern the query loop already uses for an abandoned call
  (#10035); a residual issue where the wrong-password and
  unknown-user cases in that same retry sequence report the
  wrong specific ORA error number, without hanging or otherwise
  affecting the connection, is filed separately (#10039)
- fixed the oracle protocol module reporting the wrong specific ORA
  error number (ORA-03120/ORA-24327 instead of ORA-01017) for
  a wrong password or unknown user in a login-retry sequence on
  one connection; sendErrorPacket() was sending every non-o3logon
  client the login-failure summary object shaped for an old OCI7
  client, one field short in front of the ORA number, so a modern
  client misread the shift.  now sends the shape a real 11.2+
  server does, confirmed against a live capture of a real Oracle
  12.2 server and against the module's own output (#10039)
- fixed the oracle protocol module answering an oci7 oexfet() with every
  row left in the result set rather than the nrows it asked
  for, overrunning the client's define buffers.  the count
  rides in the query2 request's descriptor block rather than
  its header, and nothing had read it (#10030)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy oci7 fetch path dropping
  every row of a batch past the first packet.  a legacy fetch
  is one call and one answer, so a client has no way to ask for
  the rest - a real server sends the whole batch as one ttc
  byte stream and lets the tns layer split it at the sdu, which
  is what the module does now (#10030)
- fixed the oracle protocol module ending a short oci7 fetch batch
  cleanly.  a batch that asks for more rows than the result set
  has left gets the rows it could fill and ORA-01403 together,
  which is how the client learns it has reached the end (#10030)
- fixed the oracle protocol module reporting every oci7 statement as a
  select.  the command type in the summary object was a literal
  3, so an insert, update, delete or pl/sql block all came back
  in the client's cda.ft as OTYSEL.  the statement is classified
  now (#10030)
- fixed the db2 connection module corrupting multibyte clob data on a
  multi-segment fetch.  getLobFieldSegment mistook the caller's
  byte offset for db2's character-based read position, which
  drifts apart for multibyte data; it now tracks its own
  character-position cursor for a sequential read, falling back
  to the old byte-as-position behavior otherwise (#10040)
- fixed the oracle connection module reading a described column's scale
  two bytes wide when oracle only writes one.  precision really
  is 2 bytes and scale really is 1 - the widths OCIAttrGet's own
  size out-parameter reports - so the 2-byte read left scale in
  the high byte on a big-endian host (solaris8sparc) and
  reported 0 for every column that had one, eg. a NUMBER(10,2)
  described as scale 0 to an oci7 client.  no change on
  little-endian, where the byte lands in the low half either
  way (#10033)
- fixed the oracle protocol module deadlocking a modern client that
  wire-speaks the legacy oci7 call interface under a
  verifiertype="9i" listener.  the module answered its
  data-type-negotiation request in the old ub1 field width
  unconditionally under verifiertype="9i", assuming that meant
  a genuinely old client; this client's array carries the ub2
  width instead, same as every other modern client, so it broke
  the call and then blocked reading a result that never came.
  the width, and a client's request for the db time zone
  version field, are read off the client's own request now
  instead of off verifiertype (#10042)
- fixed the oracle protocol module hanging a modern client that
  wire-speaks the legacy oci7 call interface - instant client 23
  among them - logging in under a verifiertype="9i" listener. the
  module never read the client's CCAP_LOGON_TYPES capability
  byte, so it always answered with the old 16 byte des session
  key and summary object a genuine 1990s oci7 client expects; a
  client advertising the newer o5logon capability wants a 32 byte
  aes-128 session key and a wider summary object instead, and
  blocked waiting for bytes that never came. the module now reads
  that capability byte and answers with whichever o3logon variant
  the client asked for, while a genuine old client still gets
  exactly what it got before (#10048)
- fixed the oracle protocol module hanging the same o5logon-capable
  legacy oci7 clients (#10048) on their first describe call once
  login was fixed. their describe response needs an extra count
  field, the column's own 0-based index, after each column's
  metadata; a genuine old oci7 client's describe is unaffected
  (#10051)
- fixed the oracle protocol module miscomputing the trailing
  string-blob length for a classic pre-o5logon oci7 login
  (verifiertype="9i" only). it read the terminal name's length
  off the wire but discarded it instead of adding it into the
  blob length, so a client with a real controlling terminal -
  ttyname() populates it, not an olog() argument - got its
  username and everything after it misparsed from the wrong
  offset, dropping the connection (ORA-03113) instead of logging
  in or answering with the right auth failure (#10049)
- fixed the oracle protocol module dropping every bind value and
  desyncing the session on a query3 request whose row data
  block started exactly on a tns packet boundary.
  getQuery3BindValues()'s block walk tested for a byte in hand
  and the block's own marker byte together, so a boundary
  landing there exited the walk having read zero blocks even
  though the wire's own iteration count already said more were
  coming; it now refills for that case, and leaves alone the
  case where a client claims 0 iterations but sends row data
  anyway. the same walk is shared by reexecute(), where
  refilling unconditionally would have made an ordinary
  re-execute of a bindless statement wait out a
  continuationtimeout instead of returning at once; it now
  returns immediately when there are no binds to read (#10050)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's query3 and fetch response
  builders silently dropping rows on a wide array fetch. each
  one capped a response to one physical tns packet's worth of
  rows and stashed the rest to send first on a continuation
  fetch, but a real oracle client only performs one automatic
  continuation fetch per array-fetch call - so a batch needing
  more than one continuation lost rows outright instead of
  erroring. sendPacket() has been able to split an oversized
  logical response across several physical packets since
  #9989; the two builders now pack the whole requested batch
  into one logical response and let it do the splitting,
  matching what the legacy fetch response builder already did
  (#10030) (#10056)
- fixed the oracle protocol module answering every re-execute of a
  bound statement ORA-01007 instead of executing it, when the
  client sends an optional trailing more-options field behind
  a bare execute's header (instant client 23's api7
  emulation) that the module never consumed (#10054)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's oci7 describe response
  breaking a modern client's connection (ORA-03146) on any
  column with a non-zero scale, by writing the scale raw
  instead of the count-prefixed form that client expects in
  the portable encoding (#10054)
- fixed the oracle protocol module silently dropping rows when a
  query2 descriptor block named more define positions than
  maxcolumncount, where it sent one packet of rows and
  reported success; it now comes back ORA-20002 before the
  statement is prepared or executed.  a block naming more
  binds than maxbindcount was already refused, but with a
  misleading ORA-01007, and now comes back ORA-20003
  (#10059)
- fixed the oracle protocol module answering a legacy bindless
  execute ORA-01036 when the sqlr-connection it landed on
  still held input binds from an earlier client session, by
  clearing the cursor's binds on the legacy parse and open
  paths, the way the query2 and query3 paths already did
  (#10063)
- fixed the oracle protocol module in osql7() and
  parseExecute() answering ORA-01036 when their pooled
  cursor retained stale defines or binds from an earlier
  client session, because their cursor-reset blocks were
  missing clearDefines() and/or clearParams() calls
  (#10080)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's legacy fetch response
  writing a row-header row count of 1 whenever the client
  asked for no particular count, even though the fetch
  loop kept sending rows past that until the result set or
  the packet ran out; it now writes the count that
  actually follows (#10065)
- fixed the oracle protocol module silently clamping a query3
  request's bind descriptor list at maxbindcount, where it
  installed and saved only the first maxbindcount binds and
  ran the statement anyway; it now comes back ORA-20003
  before the statement is prepared or executed (#10067)
- fixed the oracle protocol module leaving the rest of a split
  query2 request unread on the socket whenever its
  descriptor block named more defines or binds than the
  deployment allows, which made the module misread the
  leftover fragments as a new request and drop the
  session; the refusal now walks and discards the rest of
  the request instead of returning early, and any request
  the walk still can't get through ends the session with a
  malformed-packet error instead of a misread (#10069)
- fixed a narrower version of the same oracle protocol module bug: a
  query2 request whose wire bind count disagreed with its
  OPTION_BIND flag had its bind count dropped, but the bind
  descriptors and values behind it were never read off the
  socket either, so a split request could still leave a
  fragment behind for the next request's parse to misread;
  that block is now walked and discarded the same way (#10084)
- fixed the cursors and maxcursors config attributes accepting an
  unbounded value with no warning, silently wrapping into their
  uint16_t members instead (e.g. maxcursors="70000" became
  4464); both now go through a new atouint16_t() helper that
  clamps any value over 65535 down to 65535 and prints a
  warning naming the attribute and the requested and clamped
  values (#9991)
- fixed changeUser() reopening the connection's cursor pool with 0
  cursors instead of its prior count, since closeCursors()
  had already zeroed the pool's cursor count by the time
  initCursors() read it (#9994)
- fixed sqlrconfig_xmldom::parseDir() parsing every file in a
  sqlrelay.conf.d directory as configuration regardless of name,
  so backup copies and temp files silently became duplicate
  definitions; the directory parser now only parses files whose
  name ends in ".conf" (#10010)
- fixed the cursors_growby, maxsessioncount, maxbindcount and
  maxbindnamesize config attributes accepting an unbounded
  value with no warning, silently wrapping into their
  uint16_t members instead; maxbindcount and maxbindnamesize
  had no bounds checking at all, via a bare
  charstring::convertToInteger() call; all four now go
  through atouint16_t(), same as cursors/maxcursors were
  fixed by #9991; also fixed maxbindnamesize never being
  settable, since the parser checked for the misspelled
  attribute key "maxbindnamelsize" instead; and fixed
  maxcursors using the wrong fallback default
  (DEFAULT_CURSORS instead of DEFAULT_MAXCURSORS) (#10094)
- fixed atouint32_t()/atoint32_t() silently narrowing a 64-bit
  parsed value into their 32-bit return type with no range
  check; both now clamp to the 32-bit range with a warning
  before narrowing, the same way atouint16_t() already did;
  also fixed a route's port attribute silently truncating
  the same way, since it was assigned from atouint32_t()
  into a uint16_t member (#10094)
- fixed sqlrservercontroller::initCursors() printing
  maxcursorcount/cursorcount with %hd, misprinting values
  over 32767 as negative in debug output even though both
  are uint16_t and used correctly elsewhere; now printed
  with %hu (#10097)
- fixed 8 more sites in sqlrservercontroller.cpp printing uint16_t
  values (cursor ids, inet port) with %hd instead of %hu,
  misprinting values over 32767 as negative; also fixed a
  stderror.printf() call that passed two literal strings
  instead of one format string and an argument, so the port
  number was never actually printed (#10098)
- fixed the maxquerysize, maxstringbindvaluesize, maxlobbindvaluesize,
  idleclienttimeout and defaultport config attributes accepting
  an unbounded value with no warning, via bare
  charstring::convertToInteger()/convertToUnsignedInteger()
  calls with no bounds checking; all five now go through
  atouint32_t()/atoint32_t()/atouint16_t(), same as
  maxbindcount/maxbindnamesize were fixed by #10094;
  idleclienttimeout's minvalue is -1 to preserve its "wait
  forever" sentinel value; also renamed the documented
  "maxbindvars" config attribute to "maxbindcount", its real,
  long-existing name, since maxbindvars was never actually
  read by any config parser (#10096)
- fixed the postgresql and oracle protocol modules' debug output
  passing a NULL column type name straight into a %s
  conversion when getColumnTypeName() (postgresql) or the
  describe path (oracle, via debugColumnType()) returned
  NULL; both now fall back to "(unknown)" (#10024)
- fixed the oracle protocol module's isCharacterColumn() treating
  a NULL columntypestring as a character column instead of
  not one, the same way an unrecognized "UNKNOWN" type name
  already was; affects the column size reported to the
  client on describe (#10024)
- fixed the postgresql protocol module's getQuery() hardcoding
  mysql/mariadb backslash-escaping rules when scanning quoted
  strings/identifiers, even though this protocol module can
  front a mysql/mariadb backend too (see the
  mysqlpostgresqlprotocol test instance); it now bases that
  on the connection's native db type instead (#7433)
- fixed the sap connection module's rewriteBindMarkersToPositional()
  quote-tracking: it treated a backslash before a quote as an
  escape and a doubled quote as closing one string literal and
  opening another, but sybase/ase has no backslash-escaping in
  string literals and represents an embedded literal quote by
  doubling it; it now jumps over each literal with
  charstring::findEndOfQuotedString() instead of toggling an
  inquotes flag on every unescaped quote (#7433)
- fixed sqlrservercontroller::getFirstValuesFromInsertQuery()
  hardcoding mysql/mariadb backslash-escaping rules when
  scanning quoted string literals in an insert statement's
  values, even though the function is reached for other
  backends too; it now bases that on the connection's
  native db type. also fixed the scan having no bound check
  against the end of the query, so a malformed value list
  could send it reading past the end of the buffer (#7433)
- fixed the replay trigger's appendValues() hardcoding mysql/mariadb
  backslash-escaping rules when scanning quoted string literals
  in an insert statement's values, now based on the connection's
  native db type instead. also fixed the scan having no bound
  check against the end of the query, so a malformed value list
  could send it reading past the end of the buffer (#7433)
- fixed the splitmultiinsert trigger's parseValues() hardcoding
  mysql/mariadb backslash-escaping rules when scanning quoted
  string literals in a multi-insert's value sets, even though
  the trigger isn't restricted to those backends; it now bases
  that on the connection's native db type. also fixed the scan
  having no bound check against the end of the query, so a
  malformed value list could send it reading past the end of
  the buffer (#7433)
- fixed the sequence_nextval query translation module's run()
  hardcoding mysql/mariadb backslash-escaping rules when
  copying string literals verbatim, even though the module
  isn't restricted to a single backend; it now bases that on
  the connection's native db type (#7433)
- fixed the listagg_to_string_agg and keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg
  query translation modules, and sqlrservercursor's
  containsOnCommitPreserveRows(), hardcoding mysql/mariadb
  backslash-escaping rules when scanning quoted string literals,
  even though none of them can ever run against a mysql/mariadb
  backend (#7433)
- fixed sqlrservercursor's fakeInputBinds(), sqlrservercontroller's
  translateBindVariables(), and the oracle protocol module's
  getBindVariableName() hardcoding mysql/mariadb
  backslash-escaping rules while scanning a query for bind
  variables, even though all three are reached for other
  backends too; a literal ending in a backslash right before a
  quote could swallow the quote, and anything after it
  including a later bind variable. they now base that on the
  connection's native db type instead (#7433)
- fixed an out-of-bounds read in the c++ api's
  sqlrcursor::performSubstitutionsInternal(): its quote-tracking
  check looked at the byte before the current one on every
  iteration, including the first, reading one byte before the
  start of the query buffer (#7433)
- fixed the php pdo driver's sqlrconnectionRewriteQuery() dropping
  the last bind variable in a query when that variable ran to
  the very end of the query with no trailing delimiter to close
  it, e.g. "...where id=:id" (#7433)
- fixed the normalize query translation module only recognizing a
  line comment when "--" was followed by a space, which let a
  comment written without the space (e.g. "--foo") fall through
  unstripped and get merged onto the same line by the
  whitespace-collapse pass that follows, silently commenting out
  the rest of the statement once it reached the database; also
  added handling for "/* */" block comments, which it didn't
  recognize at all before, letting a block comment containing a
  quote desync its quoted-string tracking; a removed comment now
  also collapses into a single separating space rather than
  gluing the surrounding tokens together or leaving a double
  space; on mysql/mariadb, a bare "--" still requires trailing
  whitespace (or end of statement) to start a comment, matching
  that backend's own lexer, since mysql treats something like
  "5--3" as arithmetic rather than a comment (#9968)
- fixed the replay trigger's appendValues() dropping the closing
  quote from every quoted insert value it rewrote, turning
  insert into t values ('a','b',3) into
  insert into t values ('a,'b,3), which is invalid sql; the
  value.append() call was copying one byte short (#10102)
- fixed the same uint16_t/%hd misprinting bug #10097 and #10098
  fixed in sqlrservercontroller.cpp, at further sites across
  the firebird, mysql, postgresql, sqlrclient, tds and
  teradata protocol modules, sqlrschedule.cpp, and the upsert
  trigger; switched to %hu (#10099)
- fixed the c++ api's sqlrclientcolumn class having no constructor,
  leaving its 18 members uninitialized until the wire-parse path
  populated them, which could leak stale values from a prior
  query into a column left unrefreshed by a partial parse since
  _columns is a persistent buffer reused across queries; also
  null out typestring after it's deleted in
  sqlrcursor::clearColumns() to close off a latent double-free
  on the same reused buffer (#10100)
- fixed the replay trigger's INSERT replay to recognize when an
  auto-increment column was given a literal null and substitute
  the actual last-insert-id on replay rather than generate a new
  one; the rewriteQuery() code path was unreachable due to a
  routing bug in logQuery(), now fixed, which also exposed and
  fixed three latent bugs: dropping text after the values list,
  null pointer dereference on malformed inserts, and failure to
  recognize quoted column names (#10110)
- fixed the replay trigger's auto-increment substitution (added in
  #10110) to match a literal null case-insensitively, since
  clients don't always write the keyword in lowercase
  (#10112)
- fixed the replay trigger's auto-increment null substitution to
  split an INSERT's values list on commas only at paren depth 0,
  not on commas inside a function call's argument list, which could
  otherwise misalign the values against their columns and corrupt a
  function call's argument (#10113)
- fixed the replay trigger splicing a stale last-insert-id, left over
  from an earlier, successful insert, into an insert that itself
  errored, which collided with that earlier insert's id on replay
  (#10114)
- fixed the query parser and multi-insert-split trigger to recognize
  INSERT, VALUES, and SELECT keywords case-insensitively (#10115)
- fixed the INSERT value splitters to recognize double- and
  backtick-quoted literals, not just single-quoted ones (#10117)
- fixed the upsert trigger to refuse rewriting an insert into an
  update when a bind sits inside a value expression

### Security
- fixed an authentication hole in the des password encryption module
- fixed a pre-auth crash and an oob type map read in the mysql protocol
  module
- fixed format string injection in module debug output across the auth,
  protocol, trigger and translation modules
- fixed an authentication bypass in the sqlrclient protocol module
- fixed sqlrservercontroller::raiseDbErrorEvent()/raiseDbWarningEvent()
  running db messages through printf as a format string
- fixed passwords appearing in the clear in postgresql, mysql, tds,
  teradata and sqlrclient protocol module debug output
- guarded the same unbounded-length underflow in the sap connection
  module that was fixed in freetds; sap's row buffers are never
  zeroed, so a zero-length field there would have leaked
  uninitialized heap instead of freetds' all-null buffer had it
  ever been hit
- fixed the firebird connection module overrunning its input-bind
  describe buffers when a query has more bind parameters than
  the configured maxbindcount - it now fails the prepare with an
  error instead
- fixed the tds protocol module writing client passwords to the debug log
  - the raw packet hex dump defeated the "(hidden)" that the
  field-level debug output prints in their place, giving up a
  pre-tds7 login record's passwords outright, and login7's
  behind nothing more than a fixed xor and nibble swap
- fixed an off-by-one bounds check in the oracle protocol module's
  bindParameters() that let a bind index equal to maxbindcount
  through instead of being skipped, causing an out-of-bounds array
  access
- fixed the firebird connection module overrunning its output-bind sqlda
  when a stored procedure has more output parameters than the
  configured maxbindcount - it now fails the prepare with an
  error instead
- fixed the firebird connection module's inputBind()/outputBind()
  overloads not bounds-checking a client-supplied bind position
  against maxbindcount before writing into fixed-size arrays -
  out-of-range positions are now rejected as a bind format error
  instead of overrunning the array
- fixed the debug logger passing client-controlled text as a printf
  format string
- fixed several more format-string vulnerabilities one layer up from
  the debug logger fix - sqlrservermodule/sqlrservermodules/
  sqlrservercontroller's debugStart()/debugWrite() and several
  raise*Event() calls (parse/translation failure, schedule
  violation, client disconnected, and the oracle and router
  connection modules) were re-formatting already-rendered text
  as a printf format string
- fixed a heap out-of-bounds read in the oracle protocol module's
  query2 define handling on backends with no configured
  column limit (sqlite, router, or maxcolumncount="-1"),
  where a request that carried no define block still
  resized the cursor's define arrays to its own wire define
  count, without updating the count that bounds every later
  read of them, leaving that count pointing past a
  subsequent request's smaller arrays; such a request now
  leaves both the array sizes and their contents alone
  (#10085)
- fixed unbounded memory growth in the oracle protocol module, where
  a client could name any row count it liked in a query3
  prefetch, a fetch or a query2 request, and the whole batch
  was built in memory before any of it went out, pinning the
  connection process's memory at whatever the batch came to;
  a request over the row count limit, or a batch that runs
  past the response size limit while it's being built, is now
  refused rather than sent (#10092)
- the mysql, postgresql and oracle protocol modules now generate
  wire-visible secrets (mysql's auth scramble, postgresql's
  auth salt and cancel-request secret key, oracle's
  AUTH_VFR_DATA, pbkdf2 csk salt and fabricated challenge)
  with csprng instead of the seeded, reproducible prng; also
  added sqlrservermodule::checkCsprngIsCryptographicallySecure()
  and made every csprng-using module (the mysql, postgresql,
  oracle, tds and firebird protocol modules and the
  oracle_userlist and oracle_connectstrings auth modules)
  call it at load time, so a platform where csprng falls back
  to a non-cryptographic prng warns instead of quietly
  generating predictable secrets (#9773)

## [2.2.0] - 2026-07-27

### Added
- added some install targets for unfinished modules
- added getTableTypeList(), getTypeInfoList(), getProcedureList() to the
  sqlrservercontroller, oracle connection, and jdbc driver
- added getConnect/ResponseTimeoutSeconds/MicroSeconds() to client apis
- added set/getIsolationLevel() methods/functions
- added getDatabaseFeatures() methods/functions
- added getOutputBindDate*() and getFieldAsDate*() methods/functions
- added nextResultSet() to non c++ apis
- added selectSchema()/getCurrentSchema() methods/functions
- implemented ODBC driver SQLGetInfo() using getDatabaseFeature() calls
- added selectCatalog()/getCurrentCatalog() methods/functions
- added getDatabaseIsSchema() methods/functions and abstracted
  "database" to be either catalog or schema, depending on how
  the database backend interprets it
- added proper erlang docs
- added jdbc driver to redhat/debian packaging
- added getCurrentUser() and implemented odbc SQL_USER_NAME with it
- added odbc and jdbc programming docs
- implemented methods for getting the next result set in the various
  database abstraction layers
- added getAutoCommit() to all apis and update db abstraction layers to
  use it
- added AutoCommit yes/no/backend/"" connect string option to ODBC driver
- added binary-compatible sendQuery to C# api
- added MapDateTimeToDate, MapDateToTimeStamp, and MapNewDateToTimeStamp
  options to ODBC
- implemented postgresql getColumnScale()
- implemented postgresql getColumnSize() for variable-length types
- implemented postgresql isColumnNullable() (and made it optional)
- added AutoCommit yes/no/backend connect string option to JDBC driver
- added autocommit 1/0/-1 connect string option to PHP PDO driver
- added lazyconnectautocommit connect string option to PHP PDO driver
- added sqlite busytimeoutms option to allow handle transient write lock
  contention
- added getInTransaction() api call
- added decodeblobs connect string option, defaulting true
- added the savepoints trigger module
- added globaltemptables trigger module
- added listagg_to_string_agg query translation module
- implemented getColumnIsNullable() for sqlite
- added LVARCHAR data type
- added sequence_nextval query translation module
- odbc driver's SQLGetEnvAttr() now supports the Progress
  SQL_ATTR_APP_WCHAR_TYPE, SQL_ATTR_APP_UNICODE_TYPE, and
  SQL_ATTR_DRIVER_UNICODE_TYPE environment attributes, returning
  their values as a SQLPOINTER
- sap and freetds connections now support a csversion connect string
  option, which defaults to the current version, and will fall
  back to lower versions as needed
- added DO-NOT-EDIT banners to non-installed make-generated files
- added shared containsOnCommitPreserveRows()/
  setOnCommitPreserveRowsPattern() to the server cursor
- sqlrcrud now accepts XML-formatted criteria and sort in addition to
  JSON
- added dependency info to the debian/ubuntu package control file
- added a Py_ssize_t compatibility typedef for python < 2.5
- added a RARRAY_LEN compatitility macro for ruby < 1.8.6
- added parseNumber(SQLUINTEGER) casts for older gcc
- added the rocky->r and win->w rules to the SHORTHOSTNAME map so it
  mirrors the buildfarm db2 short-name map - db2 database names are
  limited to 8 characters, and the db2 test's db name comes from
  SHORTHOSTNAME, so a 9-char host like rocky8x64 got db=rocky8x64
  (invalid, and not the cataloged r8x64) and the db2 connect failed

### Changed
- jdbc driver debugging improvements
- general java api and jdbc driver improvements
- improved getColumnListQuery() method to return auto_increment and key
  for all databases
- refactored get*List() and get*ListQuery() methods to use
  db/schema/object/component paradigm and added implementations
  for all database modules
- refactored tests a bit, replaced checkSuccess() with assert*() and
  moved assert*() into its own shared file
- pushed get-list-by-api/query logic up to sqlrserverconnection
- overhauled c++ api tests to be consistent with each other and test
  more things
- ODBC driver now maps SQL_DATE to SQL_C_DATE or SQL_C_TIMESTAMP based on
  the buffersize that was passed in
- ODBC driver SQLGetData returns SQL_NO_DATA the first time (instead of
  the second time) if we fetch past the end of a string/lob
- ODBC driver sets day/month to 1 rather than 0 if it can't be parsed
  out of the string
- various erlang updates and fixes
- refactored transacton model handling, added support for faking
  no transactions, implicit transactions, and various types of
  explicit transactions with transactionmodel parameter and api
  calls
- updated trigger framework with before/afterPrepare options, renamed
  before/after to before/afterExecute
- made lang connect string option work with all dbs
- updated sap/freetds to support {call proc(...)} syntax
- updated odbc driver to use inputBindClob for all SQL_(W)LONGVARCHARs
- defaulted getColumnIsNullable() to 1
- updated informix tests to expect 1-based procedure-parameter
  ordinal positions
- postgresql connection formats doubles bound with unspecified
  precision/scale now
- odbc driver sets the indicator to the type size for fixed-size
  output binds now
- odbc driver null-terminates the SQLDriverConnect output connect
  string now
- informix connection now reports money columns as MONEY (rather than
  DECIMAL) and lvarchar columns as LVARCHAR (rather than VARCHAR),
  distinguishing them by the type name reported by the cli
- php pdo driver now coerces an unrecognized bind type to PDO_PARAM_STR
- erlang api Makefile now substitutes via $(REPLACE) instead of raw sed
- make-generated files now use a uniform out1/out2 substitution pattern
- systemd .service files generated by make instead of configure.in
- testall.sh/test.sh are now generated by make instead of configure
- postgresql getDbVersion() now consistently returns dotted version
- sqlr-connection now only runs the session start/end queries and
  restores the catalog/schema/isolation level on relogin when a
  dead connection prompted it, not at startup or load-balancer
  redistribution
- odbc SQLGetData() now returns SQL_NO_DATA on a second call for a
  fixed-length or NULL column rather than re-fetching the value
- the controller's copyStringLiteral(), skipStringLiteral(), and
  findCommaOrCloseParen() now handle backslash-escaped
  delimiters, and findCommaOrCloseParen() now also skips
  double-quoted and back-tick-quoted string literals
- mysql connection now handles text columns as distinct TEXT datatypes
- db2 connection now drops declared global temporary tables (qualified
  with session.) and truncates created global temporary tables
  at end of session
- migrated the oracle and firebird connections to the shared
  containsOnCommitPreserveRows() and dropped their duplicate
  preserverows regular expressions
- containsOnCommitPreserveRows() now ignores string literals, delimited
  identifiers, and comments so it can't false-match inside one
- made the sqlite and firebird connections return an empty catalog
  list, matching db2/oracle and the odbc/jdbc convention for
  databases with no way to enumerate catalogs
- get*List() methods now fill in the catalog/schema columns the way the
  native odbc/jdbc drivers do: the sap, freetds, and informix
  connections return the current catalog instead of an empty
  string, the db2, oracle, firebird, and sqlite connections return
  null (those databases have no catalogs), and oracle's legacy
  user_* path returns the current schema
- the mysql connection now reports the database as the catalog and null
  as the schema (rather than the literal "def" catalog with the
  database as the schema) and the firebird connection now reports
  null for the schema, matching the native odbc/jdbc drivers;
  mysql's catalog list now enumerates the databases and its schema
  list, like firebird's, is empty
- the sap, freetds, and informix connections now gate their get*List()
  queries on the catalog parameter, returning an empty result when
  the requested catalog is not the connection's current catalog,
  matching the native odbc/jdbc drivers
- the max-statements, max-statement-length, and max-columns-in-select
  database features are now capped at the corresponding sql relay
  configured limits (maxcursors, maxquerysize, maxcolumncount) for
  all database connection modules, via a new
  sqlrservercontroller::capDatabaseFeatures() helper
- oracle now reports max_cursor_name_length per server version (30 prior
  to 12.2, 128 for 12.2+) and includes DROP_COLUMN in its
  alter_table_operations; db2 now includes DROP_COLUMN too
- the odbc driver now derives SQL_CURSOR_SENSITIVITY from the backend's
  result set types instead of always reporting SQL_INSENSITIVE
- the odbc driver's SQL_ALTER_TABLE no longer infers constraint,
  set-default and collation support from ADD_COLUMN/DROP_COLUMN;
  those operations are reported only when their own
  alter_table_operations tokens are present
- mysql now reports max_identifier_length (64)
- db2, sap, freetds and informix now report create_table_clauses,
  create_view_clauses, drop_table_clauses and drop_view_clauses
- db2, sap, freetds and informix now report index_keywords and
  value_expressions (the sap and informix native odbc drivers
  underreport value_expressions, so those are pinned per side)
- db2, sap, freetds and informix now report predicates (the sap, freetds
  and informix native odbc drivers underreport, so those are pinned
  per side)
- db2, sap, freetds, informix, firebird and sqlite now report
  relational_join_operators and row_value_constructor_expressions,
  pinned per side where the native driver underreports (sap,
  informix, firebird) or doesn't implement the infotype (sqlite)
- db2, mysql, sqlite, firebird and informix now report
  foreign_key_delete_rules and foreign_key_update_rules; db2, sap,
  mysql and freetds now report time_date_add_intervals and
  time_date_diff_intervals. where the native odbc driver underreports
  (mysql, firebird, informix, freetds) the value is pinned per side;
  the supported referential actions were verified against each
  backend (sap ase, for instance, has no foreign-key referential
  actions, so it is left empty)
- mysql, db2, sap, freetds and informix now report ddl_index_operations
  (CREATE_INDEX, DROP_INDEX) and insert_operations (INSERT_LITERALS,
  INSERT_SEARCHED, SELECT_INTO), matched to native; freetds is pinned
  per side (its native odbc driver underreports)
- db2, sap and freetds now report grant_clauses; db2, sap, freetds and
  informix now report revoke_clauses, matched to their native odbc
  drivers
- oracle now reports supports_multiple_result_sets (ref cursors,
  implicit results); matches its native odbc driver
- info_schema_views and time_date_literals now match the native odbc
  drivers (oracle, mysql and sap report info_schema_views;
  oracle and postgresql no longer over-report)
- time_date_add_intervals and time_date_diff_intervals now match the
  native odbc drivers (postgresql, mysql and freetds no longer
  over-report)
- batch_operations and batch_row_counts now report empty for all
  databases; sqlrelay executes one statement per query and
  returns a single result set, so it can't pass through the
  batches the backends support (mysql and postgresql no longer
  over-report)
- mysql now reports the time_date_add_intervals and
  time_date_diff_intervals it actually supports (its native odbc
  driver under-reports them as none) and no longer claims
  select_into in insert_operations (mysql has no SQL-standard
  SELECT ... INTO <table>; its native odbc driver over-reports it)
- getColumnListQuery() now returns a dedicated is_autoincrement column
  and the jdbc driver's getColumns() maps it to IS_AUTOINCREMENT
  (YES/NO) instead of null
- the odbc connection's get*List() results are now mapped to the
  requested mysql/odbc/jdbc format instead of always being
  returned in odbc format
- moved -std=gnu++98 out of CXXFLAGS into a separate CXXSTD variable
  applied only to the non-wrapper-api makefiles, so the language
  wrapper apis build with the compiler's default c++ standard
  (fixes the ruby api failing to build against ruby 3.0 on gcc 11)
- used uint16_t instead of c++11 char16_t in the teradata sidechannel auth
  (fixes the build under -std=gnu++98 on solaris/older gcc)
- passed (unsigned char *) rather than (const unsigned char *) to
  Tcl_NewByteArrayObj() in the tcl api (fixes the build against
  tcl 8.4 on solaris 10, whose Tcl_NewByteArrayObj() takes a
  non-const buffer)
- firebird connection now reports the detailed status-vector error
  instead of the generic sqlcode text (which hid the real cause,
  e.g. the -901 "system error" message)
- configure now auto-detects DB2 11.5 and later by scanning /opt/ibm/db2
  and /opt/IBM/db2 for any installed version, and checks lib64 (not
  just lib) for libdb2, including under --with-db2-prefix
- localized the database getting-started guides into the doc website
  (new doc/gettingstarted/ directory) instead of linking out to
  the external blog
- updated the doc website for the 2.2.0 release: refreshed the front
  page slide, bumped the download page to sqlrelay 2.2.0 and
  rudiments 2.2.0, and added the jdbc driver to the database
  abstraction layers card and page (which still described jdbc
  as bridge-only), including a new app-jdbc-sqlr-mysql.png
- switched the php api docs from phpdoc to doxygen, which already builds
  the c, c++, cs, ado.net and nodejs docs, since phpdoc
  (phpDocumentor 1.x) is long dead and won't run on modern php,
  and its makedoc deleted the docs before ever calling it
- renamed the php api's sqlrcur_substitutions() and sqlrcur_inputBinds()
  doc stubs, which carried the singular names of the functions
  above them so phpdoc dropped them as duplicates and never
  published them, and fixed the sqlrcur_getRowLengthsAssoc()
  doc stub, which was spelled "Lenghts"
- moved the php api's doc comments out of the sql_relay.doc.php stub and
  into sql_relay.cpp, above the functions they document, with a
  call-seq line carrying the php-visible signature the way the
  ruby api's docs already do; doc/api/php/makedoc now generates
  the stub with the new mkstub.pl and deletes it again, and
  mkstub.pl fails the build if a doc comment names a function it
  isn't attached to, names a function the extension doesn't
  export, or if an exported function has no doc comment
- reordered the php api's functions in sql_relay.cpp to follow the c/c++
  apis, which is the order the docs were already published in
- documented the php api's getSchemaList(), getTableTypeList(),
  getTypeInfoList(), getPrimaryKeysList(), getKeyAndIndexList(),
  getProcedureList() and getProcedureParameterList(), which were
  exported but had never been documented, and fixed the grammar
  in inputBinds()
- mkstub.pl now also fails if a php api doc comment's call-seq documents
  an arity the function doesn't accept, which is what the four
  uncallable getBindVariableDelimiter*Supported() functions were
- filled in the php api's arginfo blocks, which declared no arguments and
  no required argument count for any of the 188 functions, so
  reflection reported every one of them as taking 0 parameters;
  they now carry an entry per argument, named as the docs name
  it, and a required count, which also makes named arguments work
- moved the java, perl, python, ruby and tcl api docs into an html
  subdirectory, the way the doxygen apis already did, so every
  makedoc can clear its whole output directory before
  regenerating; each had written its output alongside its own
  script, so a blanket rm would have deleted the script, and the
  rm lists they used instead only named the file types their
  generators emitted years ago, leaving java with 33 stale files
  and ruby cleaning 6 of its 46
- each makedoc now stages what it generates, so a regen can't silently
  skip new pages the way the last one dropped 34 per-letter
  member index pages each from the cs and nodejs docs, and stops
  on a generator failure instead of staging the emptied output
  directory as deletions
- firebird connection uses fb_interpret() instead of the deprecated
  isc_interprete() for error text when configure finds it (bounded
  buffer, no overflow); falls back to isc_interprete() on pre-2.0
  clients

### Removed
- removed the redundant mixed_case_identifiers database feature and
  folded its case-sensitivity into identifier_case_storage and
  quoted_identifier_case_storage, which now also accept SENSITIVE
  (the four storage states upper/lower/mixed/sensitive are
  mutually exclusive, so one feature each expresses all of it)

### Fixed
- fixed some subtle debug attritbute-related problems
- fixed setProtocolIsBingEndian() (assignment reversal)
- jdbc driver synchronzation fixes
- fixed oracle connection's handling of catalog vs schema in its
  get*ListQuery() methods
- fixed a longstanding issue with unbound firebird output binds
- fixed a case where the postgresql named statement could get
  deallocated even though it wasn't created, which would
  immediately abort each subsequent transaction when the
  first query was run
- null and empty lob fixes
- text-encoded binary data fixes
- fixed a potential crash in the jni getRow() implementation
- java api was binding NULLs for empty strings, fixed that
- ODBC driver handles short years correctly now, when parsing dates
- fixed a bug that caused queries including binary data to be truncated
  at the first \0 in various languages (perl, python, etc.)
- fixed some lurking double-frees in the C# api
- fixed a bug in the ODBC driver where bufferlength was used instead of
  *strlen_or_ind as the size of a char/binary array for input
  binds
- fixed PHP PDO lazyconnect initialization from wrong option bug
- fixed bug that allowed faketransactionblocks=yes to be set for dbs
  that support transaction blocks natively
- fixed when="both" trigger double-free/dispatch errors
- fixed erroneous "rollback to savepoint" query interception
- fixed sap/freeds get-current-user bug
- fixed sap/freetds connection module issue where (for example) a failed
  bind could prevent queries from running until end of session
- fixed an odbc/sap bind issue
- fixed a long-standing sap select-with-bind bug
- fixed some subtle odbc/informix bugs
- fixed a db2/informix NULL-lob crash
- fixed sqlrclient cacheError() bug - missing END_COLUMN_INFO
- make uninstall no longer aborts when the init/systemd services
  aren't installed
- fixed the mysql protocol's SELECT/prepared-statement result-set column
  metadata to match native mysql: real columns now report as
  DOUBLE rather than DECIMAL, text subtypes collapse to BLOB, and
  numeric columns report native display widths rather than the
  raw precision
- fixed a bug in the mysql protocol that caused per-column unsigned and
  binary flags to be ignored
- fixed sqlrcrud's json in-list criteria to comma-separate the values
- fixed the debug logger's init() to actually set the process name and
  debug dir (dead early-returns left them null, so openDebug()
  passed null to printf %s and hung the connection on solaris)
- fixed the tcl api to build against tcl 9.0 (the CONST macro was
  removed and object/list/byte-array lengths are now Tcl_Size)
- fixed the erlang api to build under gcc 15 / c23 (colCount() is
  declared with no arguments but was being called with one)
- fixed the tcl api to load under tcl 9.0, which derives the package
  init function name from the load prefix as-is and so looked
  for sqlrelay_Init; added it as an alias for Sqlrelay_Init
- fixed the informix at-runtime build (informixatruntime.cpp was
  missing SQL_DESC_TYPE_NAME, which informix.cpp uses to read
  column type names)
- fixed the erlang binding to build with a C89 compiler (gcc 2.95)
- fixed nodejs getRow()/getRowLengths() to build against old v8
- fixed perl binding build on freebsd 15 (perl.h/libc++ pair.h) by
  adding CXXSTD to its Makefile compile rules
- fixed phppdo build with php 8.5 (REGISTER_PDO_CLASS_CONST_LONG removed)
- fixed mysql protocol test build with the mysql 8.4 client
  (REFRESH_HOSTS/SLAVE/MASTER removed)
- fixed odbc test build against iODBC / older ODBC managers (guarded the
  ODBC 3.8 SQL_DRIVER_AWARE_POOLING_SUPPORTED infotype block and the
  SQL_NULL_DESC uses, which iODBC does not define)
- fixed the doc website's Documentation button not highlighting on
  documentation.html (wrong relative path to the image) or on
  the getting-started pages (missing from the button js)
- fixed the erlang api's inputBindClob() doc comment, which had drifted
  up above inputBindNull(), leaving inputBindNull() with two
  @doc tags and breaking edoc, dropped a stray duplicate @doc
  below getDatabaseFeature(), documented getCatalogList(), and
  regenerated the erlang docs, which had fallen 101 functions
  behind while edoc was failing
- fixed the doc website's link to the c api reference from the c
  programming guide, which had one underscore where doxygen
  generates two
- fixed the php api's getBindVariableDelimiterQuestionMarkSupported(),
  getBindVariableDelimiterColonSupported(),
  getBindVariableDelimiterAtSignSupported() and
  getBindVariableDelimiterDollarSignSupported(), which were
  uncallable; each demanded 2 arguments but parsed only 1, so
  calling one with 1 argument failed the arity check and calling
  it with 2 failed the parse
- fixed 9 php api doc comments that described the wrong return value;
  8 said the function returns true or false when it returns 1 or
  0, and getDebug() said it returns 1 or 0 when it returns true
  or false
- fixed the doc website's link to the ado.net api reference, which had a
  dot where a slash belongs, and to the erlang api reference,
  which was missing the html directory
- fixed the jdbc driver's createClob() and createBlob() to return
  writable, growable Clob/Blob objects; setString()/setBytes()
  into the empty ones they used to return threw on strict
  SerialClob/SerialBlob (jdk 8u471+)
- fixed configure detection of an rpm-installed oracle instant client that
  ships only a versioned libclntsh.so (no bare libclntsh.so symlink)
- fixed the php/phppdo extension ini install dir for remi/SCL php -
  configure now takes the ini scan dir from the target php's
  PHP_CONFIG_FILE_SCAN_DIR instead of guessing /etc/php.d, which the
  SCL php does not scan (the extension never loaded, so php/phppdo
  failed for every database)

### Security
- fixed a heap overflow in the c++ api's sqlrcursor::prepareFileQuery(),
  which built its "could not be opened" error message by
  appending to an uninitialized buffer, corrupting the heap on
  the file-cannot-be-opened path in every language binding

